window.QBANK={"1":[{"i":"TCP-II-A-0001","t":"Bristol Ridge, Inc. is a loss corporation with a large net operating loss carryforward. Which event during the last three years is an ownership change that limits use of the carryforward?","mc":{"s":"Bristol Ridge, Inc. is a loss corporation with a large net operating loss carryforward. Which event during the last three years is an ownership change that limits use of the carryforward?","d":"","o":["Two unrelated investors who owned no stock bought 30% and 15% of the stock, and no other shareholder's interest changed","The corporation's founder gave 60% of the stock to her two adult children in equal shares, and no other interest changed","A single investor who already owned 20% of the stock bought another 10%, and no other shareholder's interest changed","Two unrelated investors who owned no stock bought 30% and 25% of the stock, and no other shareholder's interest changed"],"a":4,"cite":"IRC §382(g)(1); IRC §382(l); Reg. §1.382-2T","rat":"An ownership change occurs when 5-percent shareholders increase their combined ownership by more than 50 percentage points within a three-year testing period; 30 + 25 = 55 points.","ex":["The combined increase is 45 percentage points, which does not exceed the 50-point threshold.","Family members are aggregated as a single owner under the attribution rules, so there is no increase in ownership by a new shareholder group.","The increase is only 10 percentage points. The rule needs an increase of more than 50 points in the aggregate.","An ownership change occurs when 5-percent shareholders increase their combined ownership by more than 50 percentage points within a three-year testing period; 30 + 25 = 55 points."],"wt":["limit","concept","limit",""],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"A","skill":"Application","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"An ownership change occurs when the percentage of stock held by 5-percent shareholders rises by more than 50 points over a testing period of up to three years.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"TCP-II-A-0004","t":"Ridge Line Corp. had an ownership change in 2026. It has a net operating loss carryforward of $900,000, all from years beginning after 2020. The value of its stock immediately before the change was $2,000,000 and the long-term tax-exempt rate for the month of the change was 3.5%. Its 2027 taxable income before any net operating loss deduction is $300,000, and the corporate rate is 21%. What is its 2027 federal income tax?","mc":{"s":"Ridge Line Corp. had an ownership change in 2026. It has a net operating loss carryforward of $900,000, all from years beginning after 2020. The value of its stock immediately before the change was $2,000,000 and the long-term tax-exempt rate for the month of the change was 3.5%. Its 2027 taxable income before any net operating loss deduction is $300,000, and the corporate rate is 21%. What is its 2027 federal income tax?","d":"","o":["$12,600","$48,300","$56,385","$63,000"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §382(b)(1); IRC §382(f); IRC §172(a)(2)","rat":"The annual limit is $2,000,000 x 3.5% = $70,000, which is less than the 80% limit ($240,000) and less than the loss carryforward.","ex":["Ignores the ownership change limitation and applies only the 80% limit.","The annual limit is $2,000,000 x 3.5% = $70,000, which is less than the 80% limit ($240,000) and less than the loss carryforward. Taxable income = $300,000 - $70,000 = $230,000; tax = 21% x $230,000 = $48,300.","Applies the rate to the size of the loss rather than the value of the corporation's stock.","Allows no deduction for the carryforward. The ownership change limits, but does not eliminate, use of the loss."],"wt":["concept","","calc","concept"],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"A","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"After an ownership change, annual use of pre-change NOLs is limited to the value of the loss corporation times the long-term tax-exempt rate; the 80% limit also applies.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Section 382 limit = 2,000,000 x 0.035 = $70,000","Other limits: 80% of 300,000 = $240,000; NOL 900,000; lowest = $70,000","Taxable income = 300,000 - $70,000 = $230,000; tax = $48,300"]}},{"i":"TCP-II-A-0029","t":"Cobalt Ridge Corp. has an $800,000 net operating loss carryforward arising after 2017 and expects $600,000 of taxable income before any net operating loss deduction in 2027. Its stock is worth $2,000,000, and assume the long-term tax-exempt rate is 3.5%. An investor may buy stock in one of two ways. Plan A: buy 55% of the stock from existing shareholders in 2026. Plan B: buy 45% of the stock in 2026. Assume no other ownership shifts in the testing period, a 21% corporate rate, and that the net operating loss deduction is limited to 80% of taxable income before the deduction. How much more tax does Cobalt Ridge save in 2027 under Plan B than under Plan A?","mc":{"s":"Cobalt Ridge Corp. has an $800,000 net operating loss carryforward arising after 2017 and expects $600,000 of taxable income before any net operating loss deduction in 2027. Its stock is worth $2,000,000, and assume the long-term tax-exempt rate is 3.5%. An investor may buy stock in one of two ways. Plan A: buy 55% of the stock from existing shareholders in 2026. Plan B: buy 45% of the stock in 2026. Assume no other ownership shifts in the testing period, a 21% corporate rate, and that the net operating loss deduction is limited to 80% of taxable income before the deduction. How much more tax does Cobalt Ridge save in 2027 under Plan B than under Plan A?","d":"","o":["$14,700","$30,800","$70,000","$86,100"],"a":4,"cite":"IRC §382(a); IRC §382(b)(1); IRC §382(g)(1); IRC §172(a)(2)","rat":"Plan A: a 55-point shift is an ownership change, so the annual limit is $2,000,000 x 3.5% = $70,000 and the deduction is $70,000 (saving $14,700).","ex":["This is the saving under Plan A alone ($14,700), not the difference between the plans.","Subtracts the $70,000 limit itself instead of the tax it saves ($14,700). The comparison should be of tax savings under the two plans.","This is the annual limit under Plan A, $70,000, which is a deduction amount and not a tax saving.","Plan A: a 55-point shift is an ownership change, so the annual limit is $2,000,000 x 3.5% = $70,000 and the deduction is $70,000 (saving $14,700). Plan B: 45 points is not more than 50, so there is no ownership change and the 80% limit allows $480,000 (saving $100,800). Difference $86,100."],"wt":["trap","calc","trap",""],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"A","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"An ownership change of more than 50 percentage points limits the annual net operating loss use to the value of the loss corporation times the long-term tax-exempt rate; without a change, only the 80% of taxable income limit applies.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Plan A limit = 2,000,000 x 3.5% = 70,000; deduction = min(800,000, 70,000, 480,000) = 70,000; saving = 70,000 x 21% = 14,700","Plan B: no ownership change (45 points); deduction = min(800,000, 80% x 600,000 = 480,000) = 480,000; saving = 480,000 x 21% = 100,800","Difference = 100,800 - 14,700 = 86,100"]}}],"2":[{"i":"TCP-II-A-0002","t":"Tamarack Foods, Inc. had a net operating loss of $200,000 in 2025 and a net operating loss of $300,000 in 2026, and no earlier loss carryovers. Its 2027 taxable income before any net operating loss deduction is $350,000. Assume both losses arose in years beginning after 2020 and can only be carried forward. How much of the 2026 net operating loss remains to be carried to 2028?","mc":{"s":"Tamarack Foods, Inc. had a net operating loss of $200,000 in 2025 and a net operating loss of $300,000 in 2026, and no earlier loss carryovers. Its 2027 taxable income before any net operating loss deduction is $350,000. Assume both losses arose in years beginning after 2020 and can only be carried forward. How much of the 2026 net operating loss remains to be carried to 2028?","d":"","o":["$20,000","$100,000","$150,000","$220,000"],"a":4,"cite":"IRC §172(a)(2); IRC §172(b)(1)(A); IRC §172(b)(2)","rat":"The deduction is limited to 80% of $350,000 = $280,000.","ex":["Uses the 2026 loss first. Losses are absorbed from the earliest year forward.","Nets the two losses against each other instead of applying them to 2027 income.","Ignores the 80% limit and uses losses against all of the taxable income.","The deduction is limited to 80% of $350,000 = $280,000. Losses are used oldest first: all $200,000 of the 2025 loss, then $80,000 of the 2026 loss. The 2026 loss remaining is $300,000 - $80,000 = $220,000."],"wt":["concept","concept","limit",""],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"A","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Post-2020 NOLs carry forward only, are used oldest first, and the deduction is limited to 80% of taxable income computed before the NOL deduction.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Limit = 80% x 350,000 = $280,000","2025 loss used = 200,000; 2026 loss used = $80,000","2026 loss remaining = $220,000"]}},{"i":"TCP-II-A-0005","t":"Copper Bend Corp., a calendar-year C corporation, had an operating loss of $60,000 in 2026 before considering dividends. It also received $100,000 of dividends from a domestic corporation in which it owns 25% of the stock. Assume the dividends-received deduction is 65% of qualifying dividends when it applies, and that the general limit is 65% of taxable income computed without the deduction. What is its 2026 net operating loss?","mc":{"s":"Copper Bend Corp., a calendar-year C corporation, had an operating loss of $60,000 in 2026 before considering dividends. It also received $100,000 of dividends from a domestic corporation in which it owns 25% of the stock. Assume the dividends-received deduction is 65% of qualifying dividends when it applies, and that the general limit is 65% of taxable income computed without the deduction. What is its 2026 net operating loss?","d":"","o":["$0","$25,000","$60,000","$125,000"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §246(b)(1); IRC §246(b)(2); IRC §243(a)","rat":"Taxable income before the deduction is $100,000 - $60,000 = $40,000.","ex":["Applies the taxable income limit and reports no loss. The limit does not apply when the full deduction creates a net operating loss.","Taxable income before the deduction is $100,000 - $60,000 = $40,000. The 65% limit would allow only $26,000, but the limit does not apply if the full deduction would create or increase a net operating loss. The full deduction of $65,000 produces a loss of $25,000.","Ignores the dividends and the dividends-received deduction.","Subtracts the deduction from the operating loss without including the dividends in income first."],"wt":["concept","","concept","calc"],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"A","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"The 65% of taxable income limit on the dividends-received deduction does not apply if the full deduction creates or increases a net operating loss.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Taxable income before DRD = 100,000 - 60,000 = $40,000","Full DRD = 65% x 100,000 = $65,000","Loss = $65,000 - $40,000 = $25,000"]}}],"3":[{"i":"TCP-II-A-0003","t":"Alder Bay Corp. had a net long-term capital loss of $50,000 in 2026 and no capital gains that year. It had no capital gains in 2023 through 2025, so no part of the loss can be carried back. In 2027 it has a short-term capital gain of $20,000, a long-term capital gain of $10,000, and ordinary income of $100,000. It has no other capital loss carryovers. How much of the 2026 capital loss remains to be carried forward after 2027?","mc":{"s":"Alder Bay Corp. had a net long-term capital loss of $50,000 in 2026 and no capital gains that year. It had no capital gains in 2023 through 2025, so no part of the loss can be carried back. In 2027 it has a short-term capital gain of $20,000, a long-term capital gain of $10,000, and ordinary income of $100,000. It has no other capital loss carryovers. How much of the 2026 capital loss remains to be carried forward after 2027?","d":"","o":["$20,000","$30,000","$40,000","$50,000"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §1212(a)(1)(C); IRC §1211(a); IRC §1222","rat":"A corporation's capital loss carryover is treated as a short-term loss and offsets any capital gains of the carryover year, both short-term and long-term.","ex":["A corporation's capital loss carryover is treated as a short-term loss and offsets any capital gains of the carryover year, both short-term and long-term. It absorbs $20,000 + $10,000 = $30,000, leaving $20,000.","Offsets only the short-term gain. A corporate carryover is treated as short-term and offsets both kinds of gain.","Offsets only the long-term gain, which reverses the character rules.","Assumes no part of the loss is absorbed by the 2027 gains."],"wt":["","concept","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"A","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A corporation's capital loss carryover is treated as short-term and offsets all net capital gain; it is never deductible against ordinary income.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Gains available to absorb = 20,000 + 10,000 = $30,000","Remaining = 50,000 - $30,000 = $20,000"]}}],"4":[{"i":"TCP-II-A-0006","t":"Hale Rowan transfers land with a basis of $60,000 and a fair market value of $150,000 to a newly formed corporation, Rowan Holdings, Inc., in exchange for stock worth $120,000 and $30,000 in cash. Hale owns all of the stock after the exchange and the land is not encumbered. What is Rowan Holdings' basis in the land?","mc":{"s":"Hale Rowan transfers land with a basis of $60,000 and a fair market value of $150,000 to a newly formed corporation, Rowan Holdings, Inc., in exchange for stock worth $120,000 and $30,000 in cash. Hale owns all of the stock after the exchange and the land is not encumbered. What is Rowan Holdings' basis in the land?","d":"","o":["$60,000","$90,000","$120,000","$150,000"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §351(a); IRC §351(b); IRC §362(a)","rat":"The exchange qualifies under the control rules.","ex":["Uses only Hale's basis. The corporation increases the carryover basis by the gain Hale recognizes.","The exchange qualifies under the control rules. Hale realizes a gain of $90,000 and recognizes the lesser of the boot ($30,000) or the gain: $30,000. The corporation's basis is Hale's basis plus the gain he recognizes: $60,000 + $30,000 = $90,000.","Uses the value of the stock received rather than the transferor's basis and recognized gain.","Uses fair market value, which applies only in a taxable exchange."],"wt":["concept","","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"A","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"In a section 351 transfer, the corporation's basis equals the transferor's basis increased by gain recognized on boot.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Realized gain = 150,000 - 60,000 = $90,000","Recognized gain = lesser of boot 30,000 or gain $90,000 = $30,000","Corporation's basis = 60,000 + $30,000 = $90,000"]}},{"i":"TCP-II-A-0007","t":"Rita Vale transfers equipment with an adjusted basis of $30,000 and a fair market value of $100,000 to a new corporation in exchange for all of its stock. The corporation also assumes a $45,000 business loan on the equipment. What gain does Rita recognize on the transfer?","mc":{"s":"Rita Vale transfers equipment with an adjusted basis of $30,000 and a fair market value of $100,000 to a new corporation in exchange for all of its stock. The corporation also assumes a $45,000 business loan on the equipment. What gain does Rita recognize on the transfer?","d":"","o":["$0","$15,000","$45,000","$70,000"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §357(c)(1); IRC §351(a); IRC §358(d)","rat":"Liabilities assumed ($45,000) exceed her basis ($30,000) in the transferred property, so she recognizes gain equal to the excess: $15,000.","ex":["Assumes nonrecognition applies without exception. Liabilities in excess of basis trigger gain.","Liabilities assumed ($45,000) exceed her basis ($30,000) in the transferred property, so she recognizes gain equal to the excess: $15,000. Her stock basis is then $30,000 - $45,000 + $15,000 = $0.","Recognizes the entire liability as gain, ignoring the basis.","Recognizes the entire built-in gain. Only the excess of liabilities over basis is recognized."],"wt":["concept","","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"A","skill":"Application","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"In a section 351 transfer, liabilities assumed in excess of the transferor's total basis are recognized as gain.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Liabilities assumed 45,000 - basis 30,000 = $15,000 gain"]}},{"i":"TCP-II-A-0008","t":"Ana Kaur transfers equipment with an adjusted basis of $30,000 and a fair market value of $90,000 to Kaur Design, Inc. for 60% of its stock. Ben Ortiz, who transfers no property, receives the other 40% of the stock for services worth $60,000. What gain does Ana recognize on her transfer?","mc":{"s":"Ana Kaur transfers equipment with an adjusted basis of $30,000 and a fair market value of $90,000 to Kaur Design, Inc. for 60% of its stock. Ben Ortiz, who transfers no property, receives the other 40% of the stock for services worth $60,000. What gain does Ana recognize on her transfer?","d":"","o":["$0","$30,000","$60,000","$90,000"],"a":3,"cite":"IRC §351(a); IRC §368(c); Reg. §1.351-1(a)(1)","rat":"Stock issued for services does not count toward the 80% control test.","ex":["Assumes the exchange qualifies for nonrecognition. Control requires the property transferors to own at least 80%.","Reports her basis as the gain. The gain is the excess of value over basis.","Stock issued for services does not count toward the 80% control test. Ana, the only transferor of property, owns only 60% immediately after the exchange, so section 351 does not apply. She recognizes the full gain, $90,000 - $30,000 = $60,000.","Treats the whole amount realized as gain and ignores her basis."],"wt":["concept","calc","","calc"],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"A","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Stock issued for services is not counted in the 80% control group; if property transferors own less than 80%, the transfer is a taxable exchange.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Property transferor's ownership = 60%, below 80%: taxable","Gain = 90,000 - 30,000 = $60,000"]}}],"5":[{"i":"TCP-II-A-0009","t":"Fenwick Tool Corp., which has ample earnings and profits, distributes equipment with an adjusted basis of $90,000 and a fair market value of $60,000 to its sole shareholder, Ella Frank, as a dividend. What are Ella's dividend income and her basis in the equipment?","mc":{"s":"Fenwick Tool Corp., which has ample earnings and profits, distributes equipment with an adjusted basis of $90,000 and a fair market value of $60,000 to its sole shareholder, Ella Frank, as a dividend. What are Ella's dividend income and her basis in the equipment?","d":"","o":["Dividend of $60,000 and a basis of $60,000","Dividend of $90,000 and a basis of $90,000","Dividend of $60,000 and a basis of $90,000","Dividend of $30,000 and a basis of $60,000"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §301(b)(1); IRC §301(d); IRC §311(a)","rat":"A corporation recognizes no loss on a distribution of property that has declined in value.","ex":["A corporation recognizes no loss on a distribution of property that has declined in value. The shareholder's dividend and basis are the property's fair market value, $60,000.","Uses the corporation's adjusted basis. A shareholder is taxed on and takes basis equal to fair market value.","Takes the dividend at fair market value but carries over the corporation's basis. The shareholder's basis is fair market value.","Treats only the decline in value as the dividend."],"wt":["","concept","concept","calc"],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"A","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A shareholder is taxed on a property distribution at fair market value and takes that value as basis; the corporation recognizes no loss.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Dividend = fair market value = 60,000","Shareholder's basis = 60,000; corporation's loss of 30,000 is not recognized"]}},{"i":"TCP-II-A-0010","t":"Granite Bluff Corp., which has ample earnings and profits, distributes land with a basis of $30,000 and a fair market value of $120,000, subject to a $50,000 mortgage that the shareholder assumes, to its sole shareholder. The corporate rate is 21%. What is the amount of the shareholder's dividend, and what federal income tax does the corporation owe on the gain from the distribution?","mc":{"s":"Granite Bluff Corp., which has ample earnings and profits, distributes land with a basis of $30,000 and a fair market value of $120,000, subject to a $50,000 mortgage that the shareholder assumes, to its sole shareholder. The corporate rate is 21%. What is the amount of the shareholder's dividend, and what federal income tax does the corporation owe on the gain from the distribution?","d":"","o":["Dividend of $120,000; corporate tax of $18,900","Dividend of $70,000; corporate tax of $18,900","Dividend of $70,000; corporate tax of $8,400","Dividend of $90,000; corporate tax of $18,900"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §311(b); IRC §301(b)(2); IRC §11(b)","rat":"The corporation recognizes gain of $120,000 - $30,000 = $90,000, taxed at 21% = $18,900.","ex":["Ignores the mortgage. The distribution is reduced by the liability the shareholder assumes.","The corporation recognizes gain of $120,000 - $30,000 = $90,000, taxed at 21% = $18,900. The amount distributed is fair market value reduced by the liability assumed: $70,000.","Reduces the corporation's gain by the mortgage. The gain is measured against fair market value, not net equity.","Treats the corporation's gain as the shareholder's dividend."],"wt":["concept","","concept","trap"],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"A","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A property distribution triggers corporate gain on fair market value; the shareholder's dividend equals fair market value less liabilities assumed.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Corporate gain = 120,000 - 30,000 = $90,000; tax = $18,900","Dividend = 120,000 - 50,000 = $70,000"]}},{"i":"TCP-II-A-0028","t":"Harbor Tool Corp., a C corporation with ample earnings and profits taxed at a flat 21%, will transfer $250,000 of value to its sole shareholder. It owns Asset X, worth $150,000 with an adjusted basis of $50,000, and Asset Y, worth $100,000 with an adjusted basis of $160,000. Both are business assets held more than a year, and any net gain or loss is taxed or deducted at 21%. Plan 1: distribute both assets in kind. Plan 2: sell Asset Y to an unrelated buyer for $100,000 in cash, then distribute Asset X and the $100,000 of cash. The shareholder receives a $250,000 dividend either way. How much less total corporate income tax does Plan 2 produce than Plan 1?","mc":{"s":"Harbor Tool Corp., a C corporation with ample earnings and profits taxed at a flat 21%, will transfer $250,000 of value to its sole shareholder. It owns Asset X, worth $150,000 with an adjusted basis of $50,000, and Asset Y, worth $100,000 with an adjusted basis of $160,000. Both are business assets held more than a year, and any net gain or loss is taxed or deducted at 21%. Plan 1: distribute both assets in kind. Plan 2: sell Asset Y to an unrelated buyer for $100,000 in cash, then distribute Asset X and the $100,000 of cash. The shareholder receives a $250,000 dividend either way. How much less total corporate income tax does Plan 2 produce than Plan 1?","d":"","o":["$8,400","$12,600","$21,000","$33,600"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §311(a); IRC §311(b); IRC §1231(a)","rat":"Plan 1: the corporation recognizes the $100,000 gain on Asset X and cannot deduct the $60,000 loss on Asset Y, so tax is $21,000.","ex":["This is the corporate tax under Plan 2, not the difference between the plans.","Plan 1: the corporation recognizes the $100,000 gain on Asset X and cannot deduct the $60,000 loss on Asset Y, so tax is $21,000. Plan 2: the loss on the sale of Y is recognized and offsets the gain on X, so tax is 21% x $40,000 = $8,400. The difference is $12,600.","This is the corporate tax under Plan 1, not the savings from Plan 2.","Multiplies the basis of Asset Y by 21%. The loss that is recovered in Plan 2 is the $60,000 of built-in loss, not the whole basis."],"wt":["trap","","trap","calc"],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"A","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A corporation recognizes gain, but not loss, when it distributes property to shareholders, so loss property should be sold before the distribution and the cash distributed.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Plan 1: gain on X = 150,000 - 50,000 = 100,000 recognized; loss on Y = 100,000 - 160,000 = -60,000 not recognized; tax = 21% x 100,000 = 21,000","Plan 2: net gain = 100,000 - 60,000 = 40,000; tax = 21% x 40,000 = 8,400","Difference = 21,000 - 8,400 = 12,600"]}},{"i":"TCP-II-A-0030","t":"Basil Corp., a C corporation with ample earnings and profits taxed at 21%, owns an investment asset held for two years. The asset has an adjusted basis of $200,000 and is worth $120,000. Basil Corp. has enough capital gains this year to absorb any capital loss on the asset. Its sole shareholder wants $120,000 of value out of the corporation. Plan 1: the corporation sells the asset to the shareholder for $120,000. Plan 2: the corporation distributes the asset to the shareholder. Plan 3: the corporation sells the asset to an unrelated buyer for $120,000 and distributes the cash. In which plan or plans does the corporation deduct the $80,000 loss, and what is the corporate tax saving?","mc":{"s":"Basil Corp., a C corporation with ample earnings and profits taxed at 21%, owns an investment asset held for two years. The asset has an adjusted basis of $200,000 and is worth $120,000. Basil Corp. has enough capital gains this year to absorb any capital loss on the asset. Its sole shareholder wants $120,000 of value out of the corporation. Plan 1: the corporation sells the asset to the shareholder for $120,000. Plan 2: the corporation distributes the asset to the shareholder. Plan 3: the corporation sells the asset to an unrelated buyer for $120,000 and distributes the cash. In which plan or plans does the corporation deduct the $80,000 loss, and what is the corporate tax saving?","d":"","o":["Plan 3 only, because the other plans are a related party sale and a distribution, so it saves $16,800","Plan 1 only, because a sale to the sole shareholder at value is a completed exchange, saving $16,800","Plans 1 and 2, because the asset leaves the corporation at a value below its basis, saving $16,800","Plans 2 and 3, because a distribution is treated as a sale at value, and each saves $16,800"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §267(a)(1); IRC §267(b)(2); IRC §311(a)","rat":"Plan 1 is a sale to a more-than-50% shareholder, so the loss is disallowed.","ex":["Plan 1 is a sale to a more-than-50% shareholder, so the loss is disallowed. Plan 2 is a distribution, so no loss is recognized. Only the sale to an unrelated buyer gives a deductible $80,000 loss, saving $80,000 x 21% = $16,800.","A loss on a sale between a corporation and a shareholder who owns more than 50% of its stock is disallowed, even if the sale is at value.","Neither plan allows the loss. The sale to the controlling shareholder is disallowed as a related party sale, and a loss on a distribution is never recognized.","A distribution is treated as a sale at value only for gain, not for loss, so Plan 2 gives no deduction."],"wt":["","concept","concept","trap"],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"A","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A corporation cannot deduct a loss on a distribution of property or on a sale to a more-than-50% shareholder; a sale to an unrelated buyer produces a deductible loss.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Plan 1: related party sale (sole shareholder owns 100%), loss of 80,000 disallowed","Plan 2: distribution of loss property, loss not recognized","Plan 3: sale to an unrelated buyer, loss = 200,000 - 120,000 = 80,000, tax saving = 80,000 x 21% = 16,800","Only Plan 3 gives a deductible loss"]}}],"6":[{"i":"TCP-II-A-0011","t":"Sam Prowse owns all of the stock of Prowse Supply, Inc., with a stock basis of $50,000. In a complete liquidation in 2026 he receives $60,000 in cash and land worth $90,000. What capital gain does Sam recognize on the liquidation?","mc":{"s":"Sam Prowse owns all of the stock of Prowse Supply, Inc., with a stock basis of $50,000. In a complete liquidation in 2026 he receives $60,000 in cash and land worth $90,000. What capital gain does Sam recognize on the liquidation?","d":"","o":["$10,000","$40,000","$100,000","$150,000"],"a":3,"cite":"IRC §331(a); IRC §334(a); IRC §336(a)","rat":"The shareholder's gain is the amount realized ($60,000 + $90,000 = $150,000) less stock basis ($50,000): $100,000.","ex":["Counts only the cash. Property received in a complete liquidation is included at fair market value.","Counts only the land. The cash is also part of the amount realized.","The shareholder's gain is the amount realized ($60,000 + $90,000 = $150,000) less stock basis ($50,000): $100,000. His basis in the land will be $90,000.","Ignores the shareholder's stock basis."],"wt":["concept","concept","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"A","skill":"Application","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"In a complete liquidation the shareholder recognizes gain or loss equal to fair market value received less stock basis.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Amount realized = 60,000 + 90,000 = $150,000","Gain = $150,000 - 50,000 = $100,000"]}},{"i":"TCP-II-A-0012","t":"Windmere Corp. has two assets: a building worth $200,000 with an adjusted basis of $80,000, and $100,000 of cash. It has no liabilities other than its 2026 income tax. It liquidates completely, distributing the building and its remaining cash to its sole shareholder, whose stock basis is $150,000. Assume the corporate rate is 21% and the corporation has no other income or deductions. What gain does the shareholder recognize?","mc":{"s":"Windmere Corp. has two assets: a building worth $200,000 with an adjusted basis of $80,000, and $100,000 of cash. It has no liabilities other than its 2026 income tax. It liquidates completely, distributing the building and its remaining cash to its sole shareholder, whose stock basis is $150,000. Assume the corporate rate is 21% and the corporation has no other income or deductions. What gain does the shareholder recognize?","d":"","o":["$120,000","$124,800","$150,000","$274,800"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §336(a); IRC §331(a); IRC §11(b)","rat":"The corporation recognizes gain of $200,000 - $80,000 = $120,000 and pays tax of $25,200 from its cash.","ex":["Uses the corporation's gain as the shareholder's gain instead of the shareholder's own amount realized less stock basis.","The corporation recognizes gain of $200,000 - $80,000 = $120,000 and pays tax of $25,200 from its cash. The shareholder receives the building ($200,000) plus $74,800 of cash, or $274,800, and recognizes gain of $274,800 - $150,000 = $124,800.","Ignores the corporate tax on the gain from the deemed sale. The tax reduces what the shareholder receives.","Ignores the shareholder's stock basis."],"wt":["trap","","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"A","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"In a liquidation the corporation is taxed on gain as if it sold its assets at fair market value; the shareholder's gain is net of that tax.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Corporate gain = $120,000; tax = $25,200","Distribution = 200,000 + (100,000 - $25,200) = $274,800","Shareholder gain = $274,800 - 150,000 = $124,800"]}}],"7":[{"i":"TCP-II-A-0013","t":"Ridgemont Corp. began 2026 with accumulated earnings and profits of $150,000. It has a current-year earnings and profits deficit of $40,000 for 2026 that accrued evenly through the year. On June 30 it distributed $140,000 in cash to its sole shareholder, whose stock basis is $90,000. How much of the distribution is a dividend?","mc":{"s":"Ridgemont Corp. began 2026 with accumulated earnings and profits of $150,000. It has a current-year earnings and profits deficit of $40,000 for 2026 that accrued evenly through the year. On June 30 it distributed $140,000 in cash to its sole shareholder, whose stock basis is $90,000. How much of the distribution is a dividend?","d":"","o":["$90,000","$100,000","$110,000","$130,000"],"a":4,"cite":"IRC §316(a)(1); IRC §301(c); Reg. §1.316-2(b)","rat":"When the current year has a deficit, accumulated earnings and profits at the distribution date is reduced by the deficit prorated to that date: $150,000 - (6/12 x $40,000) = $130,000.","ex":["Subtracts more than the full-year deficit from accumulated earnings and profits.","Subtracts the deficit from the distribution instead of adjusting accumulated earnings and profits.","Subtracts the full-year deficit. The deficit is prorated to the distribution date.","When the current year has a deficit, accumulated earnings and profits at the distribution date is reduced by the deficit prorated to that date: $150,000 - (6/12 x $40,000) = $130,000. The distribution is a dividend to that extent, so $130,000; the remaining $10,000 is a return of basis."],"wt":["calc","calc","calc",""],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"A","skill":"Application","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"With a current deficit and positive accumulated E&P, the deficit is prorated to the distribution date to measure accumulated E&P available for dividends.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Accumulated E&P at June 30 = 150,000 - 0.5 x 40,000 = $130,000","Dividend = lesser of 140,000 and $130,000 = $130,000","Return of basis = $10,000"]}},{"i":"TCP-II-A-0014","t":"Pinegate Corp. distributed $135,000 in cash to its sole shareholder in 2026. The corporation had current earnings and profits of $70,000 and accumulated earnings and profits of $20,000. The shareholder's stock basis was $30,000. What capital gain does the shareholder recognize?","mc":{"s":"Pinegate Corp. distributed $135,000 in cash to its sole shareholder in 2026. The corporation had current earnings and profits of $70,000 and accumulated earnings and profits of $20,000. The shareholder's stock basis was $30,000. What capital gain does the shareholder recognize?","d":"","o":["$0","$15,000","$45,000","$105,000"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §301(c)(1)-(3); IRC §316(a); IRC §1001","rat":"Dividends come first from current and accumulated earnings and profits ($70,000 + $20,000 = $90,000).","ex":["Assumes the entire distribution is covered by earnings and profits or basis.","Dividends come first from current and accumulated earnings and profits ($70,000 + $20,000 = $90,000). The next $30,000 is a tax-free return of basis, reducing basis to $0, and the remaining $15,000 is capital gain.","Treats all of the distribution above earnings and profits as gain without first reducing basis.","Ignores earnings and profits and taxes only the excess over basis."],"wt":["concept","","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"A","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Distributions are dividends to the extent of E&P, then a return of basis, then capital gain.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Dividend = $90,000","Return of basis = $30,000","Gain = 135,000 - $90,000 - $30,000 = $15,000"]}},{"i":"TCP-II-A-0031","t":"Ray Ostrom owns 60% of the stock of Ostrom Co., a C corporation with ample earnings and profits. His son Sam owns the other 40%. Ray's basis in his shares is $100,000 and he can get $500,000 for them. Plan 1: the corporation redeems all of Ray's shares and no agreement is filed. Plan 2: the corporation redeems all of Ray's shares, and Ray files the required agreement, will hold no interest in the corporation other than as a creditor (not as an officer, director, or employee) for 10 years, and has not transferred stock to Sam or received stock from Sam in the previous 10 years. Plan 3: Ray sells all of his shares to Sam for $500,000. Assume qualified dividends and long-term capital gain are both taxed at 20%. What is Ray's federal income tax on each plan?","mc":{"s":"Ray Ostrom owns 60% of the stock of Ostrom Co., a C corporation with ample earnings and profits. His son Sam owns the other 40%. Ray's basis in his shares is $100,000 and he can get $500,000 for them. Plan 1: the corporation redeems all of Ray's shares and no agreement is filed. Plan 2: the corporation redeems all of Ray's shares, and Ray files the required agreement, will hold no interest in the corporation other than as a creditor (not as an officer, director, or employee) for 10 years, and has not transferred stock to Sam or received stock from Sam in the previous 10 years. Plan 3: Ray sells all of his shares to Sam for $500,000. Assume qualified dividends and long-term capital gain are both taxed at 20%. What is Ray's federal income tax on each plan?","d":"","o":["Plan 1: $100,000; Plan 2: $80,000; Plan 3: $80,000","Plan 1: $80,000; Plan 2: $80,000; Plan 3: $80,000","Plan 1: $100,000; Plan 2: $100,000; Plan 3: $80,000","Plan 1: $100,000; Plan 2: $80,000; Plan 3: $100,000"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §302(b)(3); IRC §302(c)(2); IRC §318(a)(1); IRC §301(c)","rat":"In Plan 1 Ray is treated as owning Sam's shares, so after the redemption he still owns 100% and the payment is a $500,000 dividend ($100,000).","ex":["In Plan 1 Ray is treated as owning Sam's shares, so after the redemption he still owns 100% and the payment is a $500,000 dividend ($100,000). In Plan 2 the waiver of attribution lets the redemption count as a complete termination and gives a $400,000 gain ($80,000). In Plan 3 the sale to Sam is a sale, with a $400,000 gain.","Ignores family attribution. Without the waiver, Ray is treated as owning his son's shares, so the redemption is not a complete termination and is a dividend.","The waiver in Plan 2 is what allows attribution from Sam to be ignored, so a complete termination redemption gives capital gain treatment.","A sale of stock to a related person is a sale, and gain is reported at capital gain rates. The dividend rule applies only to distributions by the corporation."],"wt":["","concept","concept","trap"],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"A","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A redemption of all of a shareholder's stock is a sale or exchange only if family attribution does not leave the shareholder with a constructive interest, which requires a waiver; otherwise the redemption is a dividend to the extent of earnings and profits.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Plan 1: constructive ownership of Sam's 40% means Ray still owns 100% after the redemption, so the $500,000 is a dividend: 500,000 x 20% = 100,000","Plan 2: with the waiver, complete termination gives exchange treatment: gain = 500,000 - 100,000 = 400,000; tax = 400,000 x 20% = 80,000","Plan 3: sale to Sam: gain 400,000, tax 80,000"]}}],"8":[{"i":"TCP-II-A-0015","t":"On January 1, 2026, Orchard Supply, Inc. lends its sole shareholder, Dev Patel, $300,000 on a demand note that bears no interest. Assume the applicable rate for 2026 is 4.0% and the loan remains outstanding for the whole year. Which statement describes the 2026 tax effect?","mc":{"s":"On January 1, 2026, Orchard Supply, Inc. lends its sole shareholder, Dev Patel, $300,000 on a demand note that bears no interest. Assume the applicable rate for 2026 is 4.0% and the loan remains outstanding for the whole year. Which statement describes the 2026 tax effect?","d":"","o":["Neither the corporation nor Dev has any income, because no interest is charged and the note is not repaid until it is called","The corporation reports no income, and Dev reports $12,000 of interest income received from the corporation on the loan","The corporation reports $12,000 of interest income, and Dev is treated as receiving a $12,000 dividend","The corporation reports $300,000 as a distribution, because the entire loan principal is treated as a dividend to Dev"],"a":3,"cite":"IRC §7872(a); IRC §7872(c)(1)(C); Reg. §1.7872-4","rat":"A below-market loan between a corporation and a shareholder is recharacterized: the corporation is treated as paying a dividend to the shareholder, who pays the interest back.","ex":["Interest is imputed on a below-market loan even when no payment is made.","The corporation is the lender, so it reports the imputed interest; the shareholder is the borrower.","A below-market loan between a corporation and a shareholder is recharacterized: the corporation is treated as paying a dividend to the shareholder, who pays the interest back.","The loan is not a distribution. Only the foregone interest is treated as a transfer."],"wt":["concept","concept","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"A","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A below-market loan from a corporation to a shareholder creates imputed interest income to the corporation and a deemed dividend to the shareholder.","from":"2026-01-01"}}],"9":[{"i":"TCP-II-A-0016","t":"Which group of corporations may file a consolidated federal income tax return?","mc":{"s":"Which group of corporations may file a consolidated federal income tax return?","d":"","o":["A domestic parent that owns 60% of the voting power and 90% of the value of a domestic subsidiary","Two domestic corporations that are each owned 100% by the same individual shareholder","A domestic parent that owns 100% of a foreign subsidiary that has no U.S. business operations","A domestic parent that owns at least 80% of the vote and value of a domestic subsidiary"],"a":4,"cite":"IRC §1501; IRC §1504(a)(1); IRC §1504(b)","rat":"An affiliated group requires a common parent that owns stock with at least 80% of the total voting power and at least 80% of the total value of each includible corporation.","ex":["Both the vote test and the value test must reach 80%.","A brother-sister group is not an affiliated group; the common parent must be a corporation.","Foreign corporations are generally not includible corporations in a consolidated return.","An affiliated group requires a common parent that owns stock with at least 80% of the total voting power and at least 80% of the total value of each includible corporation."],"wt":["limit","concept","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"A","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A consolidated return requires an affiliated group of includible corporations linked through 80% vote-and-value ownership to a common parent.","from":"2026-01-01"}}],"10":[{"i":"TCP-II-A-0017","t":"Parent Corp. and its wholly owned subsidiary, Sub Corp., file a consolidated return. Excluding the transaction below, Parent's taxable income is $500,000 and Sub's is $300,000. During 2026 Parent sold inventory that cost $70,000 to Sub for $100,000. By year end Sub had resold 40% of that inventory to unrelated customers for $52,000 and still held the rest. What is the group's consolidated taxable income for 2026?","mc":{"s":"Parent Corp. and its wholly owned subsidiary, Sub Corp., file a consolidated return. Excluding the transaction below, Parent's taxable income is $500,000 and Sub's is $300,000. During 2026 Parent sold inventory that cost $70,000 to Sub for $100,000. By year end Sub had resold 40% of that inventory to unrelated customers for $52,000 and still held the rest. What is the group's consolidated taxable income for 2026?","d":"","o":["$788,000","$800,000","$812,000","$824,000"],"a":4,"cite":"Reg. §1.1502-13(b); Reg. §1.1502-13(c); IRC §1502","rat":"Parent's intercompany gain ($100,000 - $70,000 = $30,000) is taken into account as Sub sells to outsiders: 40% x $30,000 = $12,000.","ex":["Subtracts Sub's gain instead of adding it.","Ignores the intercompany sale and the resale entirely.","Leaves out Parent's intercompany gain entirely. The gain is taken into account as the inventory is resold outside the group.","Parent's intercompany gain ($100,000 - $70,000 = $30,000) is taken into account as Sub sells to outsiders: 40% x $30,000 = $12,000. Sub's gain on the resold portion is $52,000 - (40% x $100,000) = $12,000. Consolidated taxable income = $500,000 + $300,000 + $12,000 + $12,000 = $824,000."],"wt":["calc","concept","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"A","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Intercompany gain is deferred and taken into account when the property leaves the group (matching rule).","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Parent's gain taken into account = 40% x $30,000 = $12,000","Sub's gain on resale = 52,000 - 40% x 100,000 = $12,000","Total = 500,000 + 300,000 + $12,000 + $12,000 = $824,000"]}},{"i":"TCP-II-A-0018","t":"Parent Corp. and Sub Corp. file a consolidated return for 2026. Parent has separate taxable income of $300,000 and Sub has a separate net operating loss of $60,000. What is the group's 2026 federal income tax at 21%?","mc":{"s":"Parent Corp. and Sub Corp. file a consolidated return for 2026. Parent has separate taxable income of $300,000 and Sub has a separate net operating loss of $60,000. What is the group's 2026 federal income tax at 21%?","d":"","o":["$12,600","$37,800","$40,320","$50,400"],"a":4,"cite":"IRC §1502; Reg. §1.1502-11; Reg. §1.1502-12","rat":"In a consolidated return, members' income and losses are combined, so Sub's loss offsets Parent's income: ($300,000 - $60,000) x 21% = $50,400.","ex":["Taxes only the amount of Sub's loss.","Counts Sub's loss twice.","Applies an unrelated 80% factor to the combined income.","In a consolidated return, members' income and losses are combined, so Sub's loss offsets Parent's income: ($300,000 - $60,000) x 21% = $50,400."],"wt":["concept","calc","calc",""],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"A","skill":"Application","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Consolidated taxable income combines each member's income and loss for the year.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Consolidated taxable income = 300,000 - 60,000 = $240,000","Tax = $240,000 x 21% = $50,400"]}},{"i":"TCP-II-A-0019","t":"Parent Corp. and its wholly owned subsidiary, Sub Corp., file a consolidated return. In 2026 Parent has $300,000 of operating income, Sub has $200,000 of operating income, and Sub pays Parent a $80,000 cash dividend. Neither has other items. What is the group's consolidated taxable income?","mc":{"s":"Parent Corp. and its wholly owned subsidiary, Sub Corp., file a consolidated return. In 2026 Parent has $300,000 of operating income, Sub has $200,000 of operating income, and Sub pays Parent a $80,000 cash dividend. Neither has other items. What is the group's consolidated taxable income?","d":"","o":["$300,000","$380,000","$500,000","$580,000"],"a":3,"cite":"Reg. §1.1502-13(f)(2); Reg. §1.1502-32(b)(2)(iv); IRC §1502","rat":"Intercompany dividends are eliminated in consolidation, so the $80,000 dividend is excluded from consolidated taxable income.","ex":["Counts only Parent's operating income.","Counts only Parent's income and the dividend.","Intercompany dividends are eliminated in consolidation, so the $80,000 dividend is excluded from consolidated taxable income. The total is $300,000 + $200,000 = $500,000.","Includes the dividend as income to Parent. Intercompany dividends are excluded."],"wt":["concept","concept","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"A","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Distributions between members of a consolidated group are eliminated, so they do not increase consolidated taxable income.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Consolidated taxable income = 300,000 + 200,000 = $500,000; the dividend is eliminated"]}}],"11":[{"i":"TCP-II-A-0020","t":"Sable Systems, Inc., a domestic corporation, earned the following items in 2026. Which item is foreign source income?","mc":{"s":"Sable Systems, Inc., a domestic corporation, earned the following items in 2026. Which item is foreign source income?","d":"","o":["Fees for consulting services performed by its employees in Germany","Interest received from a domestic corporation on a bond issued to Sable","Dividends received from a domestic corporation whose stock Sable holds as an investment","Rent from a U.S. tenant leasing a building that Sable owns in Ohio"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §861(a); IRC §862(a); IRC §863","rat":"Compensation for services is sourced where the services are performed, so services performed in Germany produce foreign source income.","ex":["Compensation for services is sourced where the services are performed, so services performed in Germany produce foreign source income.","Interest is sourced by the residence of the payer, so interest from a domestic obligor is U.S. source.","Dividends are sourced by the payor's place of incorporation, so a dividend from a domestic corporation is U.S. source.","Rent from real property is sourced where the property is located, which is in the United States."],"wt":["","concept","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"A","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Services income is sourced where performed; interest and dividends by the payor's residence; rents and royalties by the location or use of the property.","from":"2026-01-01"}}],"12":[{"i":"TCP-II-A-0021","t":"Nordica AG, a foreign corporation with no U.S. trade or business, receives $100,000 of royalties in 2026 from a U.S. company for the use of a patent in the United States. There is no applicable income tax treaty. Which statement is correct?","mc":{"s":"Nordica AG, a foreign corporation with no U.S. trade or business, receives $100,000 of royalties in 2026 from a U.S. company for the use of a patent in the United States. There is no applicable income tax treaty. Which statement is correct?","d":"","o":["The U.S. payor withholds 21% of the net royalty after allowing deductions, because a foreign corporation pays the corporate rate","The U.S. payor must withhold 30% of the gross royalty, and that withholding satisfies the foreign corporation's U.S. income tax","No withholding is required because the foreign corporation has no U.S. office","The U.S. payor withholds 30% only if the foreign corporation files a return to claim the income as effectively connected"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §881(a); IRC §1442(a); IRC §861(a)(4)","rat":"Fixed or determinable annual or periodical income of a foreign person is taxed at a flat 30% of gross, collected by withholding.","ex":["Withholding is at a flat 30% of gross amounts. Deductions are not allowed against this income.","Fixed or determinable annual or periodical income of a foreign person is taxed at a flat 30% of gross, collected by withholding.","The royalty is U.S. source and is subject to withholding without regard to whether the payee has a U.S. office.","Effectively connected income is reported on a return and is not subject to the 30% gross withholding. Nordica has no U.S. trade or business, so this royalty is not effectively connected, and filing a return does not change the withholding."],"wt":["concept","","concept","trap"],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"A","skill":"Application","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Passive U.S.-source income paid to a foreign corporation is subject to 30% withholding on the gross amount unless a treaty reduces the rate.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"TCP-II-A-0027","t":"Baltic Holdings, a foreign corporation resident in a country with no treaty relief, operates a U.S. branch. In 2026 its effectively connected taxable income is $1,000,000, taxed at the 21% corporate rate, and its effectively connected earnings and profits equal that income after the regular tax. During 2026 its U.S. net equity increased by $200,000. What branch profits tax does it owe at the 30% statutory rate?","mc":{"s":"Baltic Holdings, a foreign corporation resident in a country with no treaty relief, operates a U.S. branch. In 2026 its effectively connected taxable income is $1,000,000, taxed at the 21% corporate rate, and its effectively connected earnings and profits equal that income after the regular tax. During 2026 its U.S. net equity increased by $200,000. What branch profits tax does it owe at the 30% statutory rate?","d":"","o":["$60,000","$117,000","$123,900","$177,000"],"a":4,"cite":"IRC §884(a); IRC §884(b); Reg. §1.884-1","rat":"Effectively connected E&P is $1,000,000 - $210,000 = $790,000.","ex":["Applies the tax to the reinvested amount rather than the earnings not reinvested.","Subtracts the increase in U.S. net equity twice.","Applies the 21% corporate rate to the dividend equivalent amount.","Effectively connected E&P is $1,000,000 - $210,000 = $790,000. The dividend equivalent amount is reduced by the $200,000 increase in U.S. net equity: $590,000. The branch profits tax is 30% x $590,000 = $177,000."],"wt":["concept","calc","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"A","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"The branch profits tax is 30% of the dividend equivalent amount: effectively connected E&P adjusted for changes in U.S. net equity.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Effectively connected E&P = 1,000,000 - $210,000 = $790,000","Dividend equivalent amount = $790,000 - 200,000 = $590,000","Tax = 30% x $590,000 = $177,000"]}}],"13":[{"i":"TCP-II-A-0022","t":"Which foreign corporation is a controlled foreign corporation for 2026? Each U.S. person listed owns stock directly and is unrelated to the others.","mc":{"s":"Which foreign corporation is a controlled foreign corporation for 2026? Each U.S. person listed owns stock directly and is unrelated to the others.","d":"","o":["Corporation X, owned 8% each by seven unrelated U.S. persons and 44% by foreign persons","Corporation Y, owned 45% by U.S. person A and 55% by foreign persons unrelated to A","Corporation W, owned 30% by U.S. person A, 25% by U.S. person B, and 45% by foreign owners","Corporation Z, owned exactly 50% by U.S. person A and exactly 50% by foreign persons"],"a":3,"cite":"IRC §957(a); IRC §951(b); IRC §958","rat":"U.S.","ex":["No U.S. person owns 10% or more, so none is a U.S. shareholder for the control test.","U.S. shareholders own only 45%, which is not more than 50%.","U.S. shareholders each own at least 10% and together own more than 50% of the vote or value: 30% + 25% = 55%.","The test requires more than 50%. Exactly 50% ownership by U.S. shareholders is not enough."],"wt":["limit","limit","","limit"],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"A","skill":"Application","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A foreign corporation is a CFC if U.S. shareholders (each owning 10% or more) own more than 50% of its vote or value.","from":"2026-01-01"}}],"14":[{"i":"TCP-II-A-0023","t":"Norwood Ltd. is a foreign corporation resident in a country with which the United States has an income tax treaty that follows the OECD model. Which activity in the United States most likely creates a permanent establishment?","mc":{"s":"Norwood Ltd. is a foreign corporation resident in a country with which the United States has an income tax treaty that follows the OECD model. Which activity in the United States most likely creates a permanent establishment?","d":"","o":["Using a leased warehouse solely to store and deliver goods that it owns to its customers in the United States","Maintaining an office solely to purchase goods for its own use and for resale by it in its home country","Keeping a stock of goods solely for processing by another enterprise in the United States for the corporation","Maintaining a fixed office where its employees negotiate and habitually conclude sales contracts"],"a":4,"cite":"Treaty OECD Model Art. 5; IRC §864(b); IRC §894(a)","rat":"A fixed place of business through which the enterprise carries on business, including habitual conclusion of contracts, is a permanent establishment.","ex":["Storage or delivery of goods solely for the enterprise is a listed exception under the model treaty.","A fixed place used only for purchasing is a preparatory or auxiliary activity that does not create a permanent establishment.","Stock held solely for processing by another enterprise is excluded from permanent establishment status.","A fixed place of business through which the enterprise carries on business, including habitual conclusion of contracts, is a permanent establishment."],"wt":["concept","concept","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"A","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A permanent establishment is a fixed place of business, or a dependent agent who habitually concludes contracts; storage, purchasing, and other preparatory activities are excluded.","from":"2026-01-01"}}],"15":[{"i":"TCP-II-A-0024","t":"Crestview Corp., a domestic C corporation with substantial U.S. income, plans to start a business in a foreign country and expects to lose $200,000 in the first year. Which structure allows Crestview to deduct that first-year loss against its U.S. income currently?","mc":{"s":"Crestview Corp., a domestic C corporation with substantial U.S. income, plans to start a business in a foreign country and expects to lose $200,000 in the first year. Which structure allows Crestview to deduct that first-year loss against its U.S. income currently?","d":"","o":["Operating through a foreign branch, because the branch's loss is part of Crestview's own taxable income","Operating through a wholly owned foreign subsidiary corporation, because a parent may deduct a subsidiary's losses","Operating through a wholly owned foreign subsidiary that files a consolidated return with Crestview","Operating through a foreign subsidiary and deducting the loss when the subsidiary's stock is later sold at a gain"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §11(a); IRC §1504(b)(3); IRC §882","rat":"A branch is not a separate taxpayer, so its results are included in the domestic corporation's return.","ex":["A branch is not a separate taxpayer, so its results are included in the domestic corporation's return.","A foreign subsidiary is a separate corporation and its losses stay within it; the parent cannot deduct them.","Foreign corporations are generally not includible in a domestic consolidated return.","A subsidiary's operating losses do not flow to the parent, and a later gain on the stock does not create a current deduction."],"wt":["","concept","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"A","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A foreign branch is part of the domestic corporation, so its losses offset U.S. income; a foreign subsidiary's losses stay in the subsidiary.","from":"2026-01-01"}}],"16":[{"i":"TCP-II-A-0025","t":"Quarry Rock Co., a domestic corporation, manufactures machinery in Ohio and sells it to customers in Mexico. Title passes to the customers in Mexico. Gross income from these sales in 2026 is $400,000. All of its production assets are in Ohio. What amount of the gross income is foreign source?","mc":{"s":"Quarry Rock Co., a domestic corporation, manufactures machinery in Ohio and sells it to customers in Mexico. Title passes to the customers in Mexico. Gross income from these sales in 2026 is $400,000. All of its production assets are in Ohio. What amount of the gross income is foreign source?","d":"","o":["$0","$100,000","$200,000","$400,000"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §863(b); Reg. §1.863-3(a)-(c); IRC §862(a)(6)","rat":"Income from inventory produced in the United States and sold abroad is sourced solely on production activity.","ex":["Income from inventory produced in the United States and sold abroad is sourced solely on production activity. All production assets are in Ohio, so none of the income is foreign source, even though title passes in Mexico.","Applies a 25% foreign share, which is not part of any sourcing rule for produced inventory.","Splits the income equally between production and the place where title passes. That 50-50 split is not part of the current rules for produced inventory; sourcing depends only on production activity.","Sources all of the income where title passes. The title passage rule applies to inventory the taxpayer purchased, not inventory it produced."],"wt":["","calc","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"A","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Income from inventory a taxpayer produces in the United States and sells abroad is sourced solely by the location of its production assets; the title passage rule applies only to purchased inventory.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Inventory produced in the United States and sold abroad: source = location of production assets (Reg. 1.863-3(b))","All production assets are in Ohio, so 100% of the $400,000 is U.S. source","Foreign source income = $0"]}},{"i":"TCP-II-A-0026","t":"Delta Manufacturing, Inc. has $300,000 of U.S. source gross income and $200,000 of foreign source gross income. It has $70,000 of deductions definitely related to the foreign income, $100,000 definitely related to the U.S. income, and $60,000 of shared expenses that are apportioned on the basis of gross income. What is its foreign source taxable income?","mc":{"s":"Delta Manufacturing, Inc. has $300,000 of U.S. source gross income and $200,000 of foreign source gross income. It has $70,000 of deductions definitely related to the foreign income, $100,000 definitely related to the U.S. income, and $60,000 of shared expenses that are apportioned on the basis of gross income. What is its foreign source taxable income?","d":"","o":["$106,000","$130,000","$176,000","$200,000"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §861(b); IRC §862(b); Reg. §1.861-8","rat":"Foreign source gross income is $200,000/$500,000 = 40% of the total, so 40% of the shared expenses, $24,000, are apportioned to foreign income.","ex":["Foreign source gross income is $200,000/$500,000 = 40% of the total, so 40% of the shared expenses, $24,000, are apportioned to foreign income. Foreign taxable income = $200,000 - $70,000 - $24,000 = $106,000.","Ignores the shared expenses. They must be apportioned between the two sources.","Leaves out the deductions definitely related to foreign income.","Uses foreign gross income without any deductions."],"wt":["","concept","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"A","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Deductions are first allocated to the income they relate to; shared deductions are apportioned by a reasonable basis such as gross income.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Foreign share of gross income = 200,000/500,000 = 40%","Apportioned shared expenses = 40% x 60,000 = $24,000","Foreign taxable income = 200,000 - 70,000 - $24,000 = $106,000"]}}],"17":[{"i":"TCP-II-B-0001","t":"Rob Yates, an S corporation shareholder, began 2026 with a stock basis of $55,000. His share of the corporation's 2026 items was ordinary income of $42,000, tax-exempt interest of $6,500, a charitable contribution of $3,000, and nondeductible expenses of $2,400. He also received distributions of $25,000. What is his stock basis at the end of 2026?","mc":{"s":"Rob Yates, an S corporation shareholder, began 2026 with a stock basis of $55,000. His share of the corporation's 2026 items was ordinary income of $42,000, tax-exempt interest of $6,500, a charitable contribution of $3,000, and nondeductible expenses of $2,400. He also received distributions of $25,000. What is his stock basis at the end of 2026?","d":"","o":["$31,100","$66,600","$70,700","$73,100"],"a":4,"cite":"IRC §1367(a); IRC §1368(b); Form 7203 instructions","rat":"Basis rises for income items ($42,000 + $6,500), falls for distributions ($25,000), and then falls for nondeductible expenses and deductions ($3,000 + $2,400): $55,000 + $48,500 - $25,000 - $5,400 = $73,100.","ex":["Leaves out the ordinary income, which increases basis.","Leaves out the tax-exempt interest, which increases basis even though it is not taxed.","Subtracts the nondeductible expenses twice.","Basis rises for income items ($42,000 + $6,500), falls for distributions ($25,000), and then falls for nondeductible expenses and deductions ($3,000 + $2,400): $55,000 + $48,500 - $25,000 - $5,400 = $73,100."],"wt":["concept","concept","calc",""],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"B","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"S corporation stock basis increases for income items and decreases for distributions, then for nondeductible expenses and losses.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Increases = 42,000 + 6,500 = $48,500","After distributions = 55,000 + $48,500 - 25,000 = $78,500","After deductions = $78,500 - 3,000 - 2,400 = $73,100"]}},{"i":"TCP-II-B-0002","t":"Maya Fenn owns all the stock of an S corporation. Her stock basis at the start of 2026 was $30,000, and she has no debt basis. For 2026 the corporation had an ordinary loss of $20,000 and made a cash distribution to her of $25,000. How much of the 2026 loss is suspended because of the basis limitation?","mc":{"s":"Maya Fenn owns all the stock of an S corporation. Her stock basis at the start of 2026 was $30,000, and she has no debt basis. For 2026 the corporation had an ordinary loss of $20,000 and made a cash distribution to her of $25,000. How much of the 2026 loss is suspended because of the basis limitation?","d":"","o":["$15,000","$20,000","$25,000","$30,000"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §1367(a)(2); IRC §1366(d)(1); Reg. §1.1367-1(f)","rat":"Distributions reduce basis before losses.","ex":["Distributions reduce basis before losses. Basis after the distribution is $30,000 - $25,000 = $5,000, so only $5,000 of the $20,000 loss is deductible, and $15,000 is suspended.","Suspends the entire loss and ignores the remaining basis after the distribution.","Reports the distribution amount instead of the suspended loss.","Reports the starting stock basis instead of the suspended loss."],"wt":["","concept","trap","trap"],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"B","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"In an S corporation, basis is reduced by distributions before it is reduced by losses.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Basis after distribution = 30,000 - 25,000 = $5,000","Loss allowed = $5,000","Suspended loss = 20,000 - $5,000 = $15,000"]}},{"i":"TCP-II-B-0011","t":"Kay Sato owns 40% of an S corporation with a stock basis of $30,000 at the start of 2026. For 2026 the corporation had ordinary income of $50,000, received $100,000 of life insurance proceeds on the death of an officer (tax-exempt), and paid $8,000 of nondeductible premiums on that policy. Kay received a $20,000 distribution. What is her stock basis at year end?","mc":{"s":"Kay Sato owns 40% of an S corporation with a stock basis of $30,000 at the start of 2026. For 2026 the corporation had ordinary income of $50,000, received $100,000 of life insurance proceeds on the death of an officer (tax-exempt), and paid $8,000 of nondeductible premiums on that policy. Kay received a $20,000 distribution. What is her stock basis at year end?","d":"","o":["$26,800","$62,000","$63,600","$66,800"],"a":4,"cite":"IRC §1367(a)(1)(A); IRC §1367(a)(2)(D); IRC §101(a)","rat":"Her 40% shares of income ($20,000) and tax-exempt proceeds ($40,000) increase basis; the distribution ($20,000) and her share of nondeductible premiums ($3,200) decrease it: $66,800.","ex":["Leaves out the tax-exempt life insurance proceeds, which increase basis.","Deducts all of the premiums instead of her 40% share.","Deducts her share of the premiums twice.","Her 40% shares of income ($20,000) and tax-exempt proceeds ($40,000) increase basis; the distribution ($20,000) and her share of nondeductible premiums ($3,200) decrease it: $66,800."],"wt":["concept","calc","calc",""],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"B","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Tax-exempt income increases S corporation stock basis; nondeductible expenses and distributions decrease it.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Increases = 40% x (50,000 + 100,000) = $60,000","Decreases = 20,000 + 40% x 8,000 = $23,200","Basis = 30,000 + $60,000 - $23,200 = $66,800"]}},{"i":"TCP-II-B-0015","t":"Lior Mizrahi owns all of Fable Co., a calendar-year S corporation. His stock basis on January 1, 2026 is $70,000, and he has no debt basis. Fable expects a $60,000 ordinary loss for 2026 and no other items. Lior plans to take a $50,000 cash distribution. Plan 1: take it in November 2026. Plan 2: take it in January 2027. Ignore any effect on 2027. How much more of the 2026 loss can Lior deduct under Plan 2 than under Plan 1?","mc":{"s":"Lior Mizrahi owns all of Fable Co., a calendar-year S corporation. His stock basis on January 1, 2026 is $70,000, and he has no debt basis. Fable expects a $60,000 ordinary loss for 2026 and no other items. Lior plans to take a $50,000 cash distribution. Plan 1: take it in November 2026. Plan 2: take it in January 2027. Ignore any effect on 2027. How much more of the 2026 loss can Lior deduct under Plan 2 than under Plan 1?","d":"","o":["$0","$10,000","$20,000","$40,000"],"a":4,"cite":"IRC §1367(a); IRC §1366(d)(1); Reg. §1.1367-1(f)","rat":"Basis is reduced by distributions before it is reduced by losses.","ex":["Assumes losses reduce basis before distributions, so timing would not matter. The ordering rule reduces basis by distributions first.","This is the basis left at the end of 2026 if the whole loss is deducted (Plan 2), not the additional loss that Plan 2 allows.","This is the loss Lior can deduct under Plan 1, not the difference between the plans.","Basis is reduced by distributions before it is reduced by losses. In Plan 1 the November distribution takes basis to $20,000, so only $20,000 of the loss is deductible and $40,000 is suspended. In Plan 2 basis is still $70,000 at year-end, so the full $60,000 is deductible. Difference $40,000."],"wt":["concept","calc","trap",""],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"B","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"An S corporation shareholder's stock basis is increased by income and contributions, decreased by distributions, and only then decreased by losses, so a distribution taken before year-end can cut off the deduction of a loss.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Plan 1: basis = 70,000 - 50,000 = 20,000 before losses; deductible loss = 20,000; suspended = 40,000","Plan 2: basis = 70,000 at year-end; deductible loss = 60,000","Difference = 60,000 - 20,000 = 40,000"]}}],"18":[{"i":"TCP-II-B-0003","t":"Lena Ong is the sole shareholder of an S corporation with a 2026 ordinary loss of $120,000. Her stock basis is $20,000. She made a $30,000 cash loan to the corporation evidenced by a written note, and she personally guaranteed a separate $50,000 bank loan to the corporation but has not paid anything under the guarantee. How much of the loss may she deduct in 2026, ignoring at-risk and passive limits?","mc":{"s":"Lena Ong is the sole shareholder of an S corporation with a 2026 ordinary loss of $120,000. Her stock basis is $20,000. She made a $30,000 cash loan to the corporation evidenced by a written note, and she personally guaranteed a separate $50,000 bank loan to the corporation but has not paid anything under the guarantee. How much of the loss may she deduct in 2026, ignoring at-risk and passive limits?","d":"","o":["$10,000","$20,000","$30,000","$50,000"],"a":4,"cite":"IRC §1366(d)(1); Reg. §1.1366-2(a)(2); Reg. §1.1366-2(a)(2)(ii)","rat":"The loss is limited to stock basis plus debt basis: $20,000 + $30,000 = $50,000.","ex":["Subtracts her stock basis from the loan amount.","Counts only stock basis. Direct shareholder loans also give her debt basis.","Counts only the direct loan and leaves out her stock basis.","The loss is limited to stock basis plus debt basis: $20,000 + $30,000 = $50,000. Her guarantee of a bank loan does not create basis until she pays under it."],"wt":["calc","concept","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"B","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A shareholder's loss deduction is limited to stock basis plus basis in direct loans to the corporation; guarantees do not count until paid.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Deductible loss = 20,000 + 30,000 = $50,000"]}},{"i":"TCP-II-B-0013","t":"Ora Lindqvist owns 100% of an S corporation that expects a $30,000 loss during 2026. Her stock basis is $5,000 and she has $25,000 of cash. Which action taken during 2026 gives her enough basis to deduct the full loss?","mc":{"s":"Ora Lindqvist owns 100% of an S corporation that expects a $30,000 loss during 2026. Her stock basis is $5,000 and she has $25,000 of cash. Which action taken during 2026 gives her enough basis to deduct the full loss?","d":"","o":["Guarantee a $25,000 bank loan to the S corporation without paying anything under the guarantee","Lend the $25,000 to her wholly owned C corporation, which lends the same amount to the S corporation","Lend the $25,000 directly to the S corporation in exchange for a written note","Lend the $25,000 to the S corporation on January 2 of the following year in exchange for a written note"],"a":3,"cite":"IRC §1366(d)(1)(B); Reg. §1.1366-2(a)(2); Rev. Rul. 75-144","rat":"A direct loan from the shareholder creates debt basis, which together with stock basis supports the loss.","ex":["A guarantee does not create basis until the shareholder pays under it.","An indirect loan through another entity does not create basis in the S corporation debt for the shareholder.","A direct loan from the shareholder creates debt basis, which together with stock basis supports the loss.","The loan must be made before the end of the year in which the loss is deducted to give basis for that year."],"wt":["concept","concept","","limit"],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"B","skill":"Application","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Debt basis arises from a direct loan by the shareholder to the S corporation, made before year end; guarantees and indirect loans do not qualify.","from":"2026-01-01"}}],"19":[{"i":"TCP-II-B-0004","t":"Kai Nguyen transfers a building with an adjusted basis of $80,000 and a fair market value of $200,000 to a newly formed S corporation in exchange for all of its stock. The corporation assumes a $90,000 mortgage on the building. What is the S corporation's basis in the building?","mc":{"s":"Kai Nguyen transfers a building with an adjusted basis of $80,000 and a fair market value of $200,000 to a newly formed S corporation in exchange for all of its stock. The corporation assumes a $90,000 mortgage on the building. What is the S corporation's basis in the building?","d":"","o":["$10,000","$70,000","$80,000","$90,000"],"a":4,"cite":"IRC §351(a); IRC §357(c)(1); IRC §362(a)","rat":"The liability ($90,000) exceeds Kai's basis ($80,000), so he recognizes gain of $10,000.","ex":["Uses the gain recognized as the corporation's basis.","Subtracts the gain from basis instead of adding it.","Uses only Kai's basis. The corporation's basis is increased by the gain he recognizes.","The liability ($90,000) exceeds Kai's basis ($80,000), so he recognizes gain of $10,000. The corporation's basis is his basis increased by the gain recognized: $80,000 + $10,000 = $90,000."],"wt":["trap","calc","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"B","skill":"Application","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Under section 357(c) the transferor recognizes gain equal to liabilities over basis, and the corporation's basis is increased by that gain.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Gain = 90,000 - 80,000 = $10,000","Corporation's basis = 80,000 + $10,000 = $90,000"]}},{"i":"TCP-II-B-0016","t":"Mona Lisle owns all of Zenith Corp., an S corporation that expects a $90,000 ordinary loss for 2026. Her stock basis at the start of the year is $10,000 and she has no debt basis. She wants to put $80,000 of value into Zenith before December 31. Plan 1: contribute long-term stock she owns, worth $80,000 with a basis of $30,000. Plan 2: sell the stock, pay 20% tax on the long-term capital gain, and contribute $80,000 of cash. She is in the 37% bracket and the loss is otherwise fully deductible. How much greater is her net 2026 tax benefit (tax saved on the deducted loss minus the capital gain tax) under Plan 2 than under Plan 1?","mc":{"s":"Mona Lisle owns all of Zenith Corp., an S corporation that expects a $90,000 ordinary loss for 2026. Her stock basis at the start of the year is $10,000 and she has no debt basis. She wants to put $80,000 of value into Zenith before December 31. Plan 1: contribute long-term stock she owns, worth $80,000 with a basis of $30,000. Plan 2: sell the stock, pay 20% tax on the long-term capital gain, and contribute $80,000 of cash. She is in the 37% bracket and the loss is otherwise fully deductible. How much greater is her net 2026 tax benefit (tax saved on the deducted loss minus the capital gain tax) under Plan 2 than under Plan 1?","d":"","o":["$8,500","$10,000","$18,500","$23,300"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §358(a)(1); IRC §1366(d)(1); IRC §1367(a)(2)","rat":"Plan 1: basis is $10,000 + $30,000 (basis of the contributed stock) = $40,000, so $40,000 of the loss is deducted (saving $14,800) and the gain is never taxed.","ex":["Plan 1: basis is $10,000 + $30,000 (basis of the contributed stock) = $40,000, so $40,000 of the loss is deducted (saving $14,800) and the gain is never taxed. Plan 2: basis is $90,000, so the full $90,000 is deducted (saving $33,300), less tax of $10,000 on the $50,000 gain, for $23,300. The difference is $8,500.","This is only the capital gain tax under Plan 2. The extra loss deduction it buys is worth more.","Counts the extra deduction of $50,000 at 37% but forgets the $10,000 of capital gain tax that Plan 2 costs.","This is the net benefit under Plan 2 alone ($23,300), not the excess over Plan 1."],"wt":["","trap","trap","calc"],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"B","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Contributed property increases an S corporation shareholder's stock basis only by the property's adjusted basis, so selling appreciated property and contributing the cash can raise the basis at the price of a capital gain tax.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Plan 1: basis = 10,000 + 30,000 = 40,000; deductible loss 40,000; saving = 40,000 x 37% = 14,800","Plan 2: gain = 80,000 - 30,000 = 50,000, tax = 50,000 x 20% = 10,000; basis = 10,000 + 80,000 = 90,000; loss saving = 90,000 x 37% = 33,300; net = 23,300","Difference = 23,300 - 14,800 = 8,500"]}}],"20":[{"i":"TCP-II-B-0005","t":"Iris Pak transfers property with an adjusted basis of $60,000 and a fair market value of $200,000 to a new S corporation in exchange for all of its stock. The corporation assumes a $40,000 liability on the property. What is Iris's basis in her stock?","mc":{"s":"Iris Pak transfers property with an adjusted basis of $60,000 and a fair market value of $200,000 to a new S corporation in exchange for all of its stock. The corporation assumes a $40,000 liability on the property. What is Iris's basis in her stock?","d":"","o":["$20,000","$60,000","$160,000","$200,000"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §358(a); IRC §358(d); IRC §357(a)","rat":"The liability is less than her basis, so no gain is recognized.","ex":["The liability is less than her basis, so no gain is recognized. Stock basis equals the basis of the property transferred less the liability assumed: $60,000 - $40,000 = $20,000.","Ignores the liability the corporation assumes, which reduces stock basis.","Uses the net value of the property rather than the transferor's basis.","Uses fair market value. Stock basis in a nonrecognition exchange is carryover basis."],"wt":["","concept","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"B","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Stock basis in a section 351 exchange equals the basis of property transferred, less liabilities assumed and boot received, plus gain recognized.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Stock basis = 60,000 - 40,000 = $20,000"]}},{"i":"TCP-II-B-0012","t":"Ned Rowe owns stock in an S corporation and has $12,000 of losses suspended from prior years because his stock and debt basis were $0. In 2026 he contributes $20,000 of additional capital to the corporation, and the corporation has a $15,000 ordinary loss for the year. Assume no other basis items. How much of his losses remains suspended at the end of 2026?","mc":{"s":"Ned Rowe owns stock in an S corporation and has $12,000 of losses suspended from prior years because his stock and debt basis were $0. In 2026 he contributes $20,000 of additional capital to the corporation, and the corporation has a $15,000 ordinary loss for the year. Assume no other basis items. How much of his losses remains suspended at the end of 2026?","d":"","o":["$7,000","$12,000","$15,000","$27,000"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §1366(d)(2); Reg. §1.1366-2(a)(5); Form 7203 instructions","rat":"His basis becomes $20,000.","ex":["His basis becomes $20,000. The total of the current loss ($15,000) and the suspended loss ($12,000) is $27,000, but only $20,000 can be deducted, leaving $7,000 suspended.","Assumes the entire prior suspended loss remains. The added basis can absorb suspended losses after the current loss.","Keeps only the current-year loss suspended, which does not match how the basis is used.","Allows no deduction, ignoring the new $20,000 of basis."],"wt":["","concept","calc","concept"],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"B","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Losses are deductible up to stock and debt basis; excess losses carry forward and are used when basis is restored.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Available basis = 20,000","Total losses = 12,000 + 15,000 = $27,000","Suspended = $27,000 - 20,000 = $7,000"]}}],"21":[{"i":"TCP-II-B-0006","t":"Tern Dunes, Inc., an S corporation with one shareholder, distributes land with an adjusted basis of $20,000 and a fair market value of $100,000 to the shareholder in 2026. The shareholder's stock basis before the distribution is $50,000. How much gain from the distribution is reported by the shareholder?","mc":{"s":"Tern Dunes, Inc., an S corporation with one shareholder, distributes land with an adjusted basis of $20,000 and a fair market value of $100,000 to the shareholder in 2026. The shareholder's stock basis before the distribution is $50,000. How much gain from the distribution is reported by the shareholder?","d":"","o":["$80,000","$100,000","$120,000","$150,000"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §311(b); IRC §1371(a); IRC §1366(a)","rat":"An S corporation recognizes gain on a property distribution as if it sold the property at fair market value, and the gain passes through: $100,000 - $20,000 = $80,000.","ex":["An S corporation recognizes gain on a property distribution as if it sold the property at fair market value, and the gain passes through: $100,000 - $20,000 = $80,000. The shareholder's basis then rises by the gain and falls by the value distributed.","Reports the full value of the land as gain and ignores the corporation's basis.","Adds the land's basis to its value.","Adds the shareholder's stock basis to the land's value."],"wt":["","calc","calc","calc"],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"B","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"An S corporation recognizes gain on distributed appreciated property, which passes through and increases the shareholder's basis before the distribution reduces it.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Gain = 100,000 - 20,000 = $80,000","Stock basis after = 50,000 + $80,000 - 100,000 = $30,000"]}},{"i":"TCP-II-B-0007","t":"An S corporation distributes land with an adjusted basis of $90,000 and a fair market value of $60,000 to its sole shareholder, whose stock basis before the distribution is $100,000. What is the shareholder's stock basis after the distribution?","mc":{"s":"An S corporation distributes land with an adjusted basis of $90,000 and a fair market value of $60,000 to its sole shareholder, whose stock basis before the distribution is $100,000. What is the shareholder's stock basis after the distribution?","d":"","o":["$10,000","$40,000","$70,000","$100,000"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §1368(b); IRC §311(a); IRC §301(d)","rat":"The corporation recognizes no loss on the distribution, and the shareholder's basis is reduced by the fair market value of the property distributed: $100,000 - $60,000 = $40,000.","ex":["Reduces basis by the corporation's basis in the land. The reduction is the property's fair market value.","The corporation recognizes no loss on the distribution, and the shareholder's basis is reduced by the fair market value of the property distributed: $100,000 - $60,000 = $40,000.","Reduces basis only by the built-in loss on the land.","Assumes property distributions do not reduce stock basis."],"wt":["concept","","calc","concept"],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"B","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A property distribution by an S corporation reduces the shareholder's stock basis by the property's fair market value; no loss is recognized.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Stock basis = 100,000 - 60,000 = $40,000"]}}],"22":[{"i":"TCP-II-B-0008","t":"Cedar Wick, Inc., an S corporation, purchased land years ago for $150,000. The land is now worth $100,000. In a complete liquidation the corporation distributes the land and $50,000 of cash pro rata to its sole shareholder, whose stock basis is $200,000. The corporation recognizes the loss on the land. Which statement correctly describes the result?","mc":{"s":"Cedar Wick, Inc., an S corporation, purchased land years ago for $150,000. The land is now worth $100,000. In a complete liquidation the corporation distributes the land and $50,000 of cash pro rata to its sole shareholder, whose stock basis is $200,000. The corporation recognizes the loss on the land. Which statement correctly describes the result?","d":"","o":["The corporation recognizes no loss on the land, and the shareholder recognizes a $50,000 capital loss on the stock","A $50,000 section 1231 loss passes through to the shareholder, and the liquidation produces no gain or loss on the stock","A $50,000 section 1231 loss passes through, and the shareholder also recognizes a $50,000 capital loss on the stock","The corporation's $50,000 loss is disallowed because the distribution is made to a related person, so basis remains $200,000"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §336(a); IRC §336(d)(1); IRC §1367(a)(2); IRC §331","rat":"The corporation's $50,000 section 1231 loss passes through and reduces stock basis to $150,000; the shareholder receives $150,000 in total, so the stock yields no further gain or loss.","ex":["A liquidating S corporation recognizes losses on distributed property, subject only to related-party and built-in loss limits that do not apply here.","The corporation's $50,000 section 1231 loss passes through and reduces stock basis to $150,000; the shareholder receives $150,000 in total, so the stock yields no further gain or loss.","That counts the same economic loss twice. The pass-through loss reduces the stock basis, leaving no capital loss.","The related-person disallowance applies to non-pro rata distributions and disqualified property. A pro rata distribution of purchased land is not covered."],"wt":["concept","","calc","trap"],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"B","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"In a liquidation the S corporation recognizes gain or loss on distributed property, and the pass-through changes stock basis before the shareholder computes gain or loss on the stock.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Pass-through 1231 loss = 150,000 - 100,000 = 50,000","Stock basis = 200,000 - 50,000 = 150,000","Amount realized = 100,000 + 50,000 = 150,000; gain or loss = 0"]}}],"23":[{"i":"TCP-II-B-0009","t":"Sun Bay, Inc., a calendar-year S corporation, earned ordinary income of $720,000 for 2026, ratably through the year. Zoe Farr owned 50% of the stock from January 1 until she sold all of her shares on May 10, 2026. The corporation made no election to close its books. Assume the 130 days from January 1 through May 10 (including the transfer date) are counted for Zoe. What share of the income is allocated to Zoe?","mc":{"s":"Sun Bay, Inc., a calendar-year S corporation, earned ordinary income of $720,000 for 2026, ratably through the year. Zoe Farr owned 50% of the stock from January 1 until she sold all of her shares on May 10, 2026. The corporation made no election to close its books. Assume the 130 days from January 1 through May 10 (including the transfer date) are counted for Zoe. What share of the income is allocated to Zoe?","d":"","o":["$120,000","$127,233","$128,219","$360,000"],"a":3,"cite":"IRC §1377(a)(1); Reg. §1.1377-1(a)(2); Reg. §1.1362-3","rat":"Without a closing election the income is allocated per day, and the selling shareholder is treated as owning the stock through the day of the sale: $720,000 x 130/365 x 50% = $128,219.","ex":["Prorates by whole months instead of by days.","Excludes the day of the sale. The shareholder who sells is treated as owning the shares through the transfer date.","Without a closing election the income is allocated per day, and the selling shareholder is treated as owning the stock through the day of the sale: $720,000 x 130/365 x 50% = $128,219.","Allocates the full year's income to Zoe even though she sold her shares in May."],"wt":["calc","calc","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"B","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Absent a closing-of-the-books election, S corporation items are allocated per share per day, with the selling shareholder treated as owning the shares through the day of sale.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Days = 130; income x days/365 = $256,438","Zoe's 50% = $128,219"]}},{"i":"TCP-II-B-0010","t":"Sun Bay, Inc., a calendar-year S corporation, earned ordinary income of $600,000 from January 1 through May 10, 2026 (Zoe's 50% holding period, including the sale date) and $120,000 from May 11 through December 31. Zoe sold all of her 50% interest on May 10. All affected shareholders consent to an election to close the books at the sale. By how much does the election increase the income allocated to Zoe compared with allocating the year's $720,000 per day? Assume 130 of 365 days fall on or before May 10.","mc":{"s":"Sun Bay, Inc., a calendar-year S corporation, earned ordinary income of $600,000 from January 1 through May 10, 2026 (Zoe's 50% holding period, including the sale date) and $120,000 from May 11 through December 31. Zoe sold all of her 50% interest on May 10. All affected shareholders consent to an election to close the books at the sale. By how much does the election increase the income allocated to Zoe compared with allocating the year's $720,000 per day? Assume 130 of 365 days fall on or before May 10.","d":"","o":["$96,164","$128,219","$150,000","$171,781"],"a":4,"cite":"IRC §1377(a)(2); Reg. §1.1377-1(b); Reg. §1.1377-1(b)(4)","rat":"With the election Zoe's share is 50% x $600,000 = $300,000.","ex":["Uses 75% of the per-day amount.","This is the per-day allocation, not the increase from the election.","Uses half of Zoe's closing-of-the-books share.","With the election Zoe's share is 50% x $600,000 = $300,000. Without it she is allocated 50% x $720,000 x 130/365 = $128,219. The difference is $171,781."],"wt":["calc","trap","calc",""],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"B","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A closing-of-the-books election allocates income by actual results to the termination date instead of by day, and benefits a seller when income is front-loaded.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Election: 50% x 600,000 = $300,000","Per-day: 50% x 720,000 x 130/365 = $128,219","Difference = $171,781"]}},{"i":"TCP-II-B-0014","t":"Ava Lund owns 50% of Kestrel, Inc., a calendar-year S corporation, with a stock basis of $100,000. On June 30, 2026 she sells all of her shares to an unrelated buyer for $500,000. Kestrel earned $400,000 of ordinary income in 2026, all of it in January through June. Ava's share of income can be figured on a per-day basis (181 of 365 days) or under the closing-of-the-books election at the sale date. Ava's rate is 37% on ordinary income and 20% on long-term capital gain. Ignore the net investment income tax. How much more total federal tax does Ava pay if the closing-of-the-books election is used rather than the per-day allocation, considering both her share of income and her gain on the sale?","mc":{"s":"Ava Lund owns 50% of Kestrel, Inc., a calendar-year S corporation, with a stock basis of $100,000. On June 30, 2026 she sells all of her shares to an unrelated buyer for $500,000. Kestrel earned $400,000 of ordinary income in 2026, all of it in January through June. Ava's share of income can be figured on a per-day basis (181 of 365 days) or under the closing-of-the-books election at the sale date. Ava's rate is 37% on ordinary income and 20% on long-term capital gain. Ignore the net investment income tax. How much more total federal tax does Ava pay if the closing-of-the-books election is used rather than the per-day allocation, considering both her share of income and her gain on the sale?","d":"","o":["$17,140","$20,164","$37,304","$100,822"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §1377(a)(1); IRC §1377(a)(2); IRC §1367(a)(1); Reg. §1.1377-1(b)","rat":"Per-day: Ava reports $99,178 of income, raising her basis to $199,178 and leaving a $300,822 gain.","ex":["Per-day: Ava reports $99,178 of income, raising her basis to $199,178 and leaving a $300,822 gain. Closing of the books: she reports all $200,000, basis $300,000, gain $200,000. The extra income of $100,822 is taxed at 37%, but it lowers the gain by the same amount, taxed at 20%. Net cost = $100,822 x 17% = $17,140.","Counts only the tax on the extra gain. The extra income is taxed at 37% and the gain falls by the same amount, so the net cost uses the difference between the two rates.","Counts only the tax on the extra ordinary income and ignores that it increases basis and lowers the gain on the sale.","This is the extra income allocated to Ava under the election, not a tax amount."],"wt":["","trap","trap","calc"],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"B","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Income allocated to a selling S corporation shareholder increases stock basis and lowers the gain on the sale, so shifting income to the seller trades ordinary income for less capital gain.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Per-day income = 400,000 x 50% x 181/365 = 99,178; closing-of-the-books income = 200,000; extra = 100,822","Extra ordinary tax = 100,822 x 37% = 37,304; basis higher by 100,822, so gain lower by 100,822: 100,822 x 20% = 20,164 saved","Net increase = 37,304 - 20,164 = 17,140"]}}],"24":[{"i":"TCP-II-C-0001","t":"Amir contributes $50,000 in cash and equipment with an adjusted basis of $30,000 and a fair market value of $80,000 to a partnership for a 40% interest. The partnership assumes a $20,000 loan on the equipment. After the contribution the partnership's liabilities total $100,000 (including the assumed loan), and Amir's share of them is 40%. What is Amir's basis in his partnership interest?","mc":{"s":"Amir contributes $50,000 in cash and equipment with an adjusted basis of $30,000 and a fair market value of $80,000 to a partnership for a 40% interest. The partnership assumes a $20,000 loan on the equipment. After the contribution the partnership's liabilities total $100,000 (including the assumed loan), and Amir's share of them is 40%. What is Amir's basis in his partnership interest?","d":"","o":["$60,000","$80,000","$92,000","$100,000"],"a":4,"cite":"IRC §722; IRC §752(a); IRC §752(b)","rat":"Basis starts with cash and property basis ($50,000 + $30,000 = $80,000).","ex":["Ignores the increase for his share of partnership liabilities.","Uses only the basis of the property and cash contributed.","Applies his percentage only to the liabilities other than the loan the partnership assumed.","Basis starts with cash and property basis ($50,000 + $30,000 = $80,000). Relief of his $20,000 liability reduces it, and his $40,000 share of partnership liabilities increases it: $80,000 - $20,000 + $40,000 = $100,000."],"wt":["concept","concept","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"C","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Outside basis = cash and property basis contributed, less liabilities assumed by the partnership, plus the partner's share of partnership liabilities.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Contributed basis = 50,000 + 30,000 = $80,000","Less liability assumed by partnership: 20,000","Plus share of liabilities: 40% x 100,000 = $40,000","Outside basis = $100,000"]}}],"25":[{"i":"TCP-II-C-0002","t":"Uma Reyes began 2026 with a partnership basis of $60,000. Her share of 2026 items was ordinary income of $40,000, tax-exempt interest of $5,000, and a charitable contribution of $3,000. Her share of partnership liabilities increased by $12,000, and she received a cash distribution of $30,000. What is her basis at year end?","mc":{"s":"Uma Reyes began 2026 with a partnership basis of $60,000. Her share of 2026 items was ordinary income of $40,000, tax-exempt interest of $5,000, and a charitable contribution of $3,000. Her share of partnership liabilities increased by $12,000, and she received a cash distribution of $30,000. What is her basis at year end?","d":"","o":["$72,000","$79,000","$84,000","$87,000"],"a":3,"cite":"IRC §705(a); IRC §752(a); IRC §733","rat":"Basis increases for income and tax-exempt interest ($45,000) and the liability increase ($12,000), then decreases for the distribution ($30,000) and the charitable contribution ($3,000): $84,000.","ex":["Omits the increase in her share of partnership liabilities.","Omits tax-exempt interest, which increases basis.","Basis increases for income and tax-exempt interest ($45,000) and the liability increase ($12,000), then decreases for the distribution ($30,000) and the charitable contribution ($3,000): $84,000.","Omits the reduction for her share of the charitable contribution."],"wt":["concept","concept","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"C","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Partnership interest basis increases for income, contributions and liability share, and decreases for distributions, losses and nondeductible items.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Increases = 40,000 + 5,000 + 12,000 = $57,000","Decreases = 30,000 + 3,000 = $33,000","Basis = 60,000 + $57,000 - $33,000 = $84,000"]}},{"i":"TCP-II-C-0003","t":"Nia Torres has a basis of $12,000 in her partnership interest. In 2026 the partnership repaid loans so that her share of partnership liabilities decreased by $30,000. In the same year she guaranteed a new partnership loan, which increased her share of liabilities by $20,000, and the partnership distributed $10,000 in cash to her. She has no other basis adjustments. What gain does she recognize?","mc":{"s":"Nia Torres has a basis of $12,000 in her partnership interest. In 2026 the partnership repaid loans so that her share of partnership liabilities decreased by $30,000. In the same year she guaranteed a new partnership loan, which increased her share of liabilities by $20,000, and the partnership distributed $10,000 in cash to her. She has no other basis adjustments. What gain does she recognize?","d":"","o":["$8,000","$10,000","$20,000","$28,000"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §752(b); IRC §731(a)(1); Reg. §1.752-1(f)","rat":"Increases and decreases in her share of liabilities are netted.","ex":["Increases and decreases in her share of liabilities are netted. The net decrease is $30,000 - $20,000 = $10,000, treated as a cash distribution. With the actual $10,000, the deemed and actual distributions total $20,000, which exceeds her basis of $12,000 by $8,000.","Counts only the actual cash distribution and ignores the net liability decrease.","Reports the total deemed and actual distributions as gain without subtracting her basis.","Ignores the increase in her share of liabilities, which nets against the decrease."],"wt":["","concept","calc","concept"],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"C","skill":"Application","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A net decrease in a partner's share of liabilities is a deemed cash distribution; gain arises to the extent total cash distributions exceed basis.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Net decrease in liabilities = 30,000 - 20,000 = $10,000","Deemed plus actual distributions = $10,000 + 10,000 = $20,000","Gain = $20,000 - 12,000 = $8,000"]}},{"i":"TCP-II-C-0015","t":"Cara Wolfe is a limited partner whose basis in her partnership interest is $50,000 at the start of 2026. Her share of 2026 partnership ordinary income is $10,000. During 2026 she receives a $40,000 cash distribution, and the partnership's repayment of a loan reduces her share of partnership liabilities by $35,000. Plan 1: make no contribution. Plan 2: contribute $20,000 in cash to the partnership on December 28, 2026. Plan 3: contribute $20,000 in cash on January 10, 2027. Which plan, or plans, avoid gain recognition for 2026?","mc":{"s":"Cara Wolfe is a limited partner whose basis in her partnership interest is $50,000 at the start of 2026. Her share of 2026 partnership ordinary income is $10,000. During 2026 she receives a $40,000 cash distribution, and the partnership's repayment of a loan reduces her share of partnership liabilities by $35,000. Plan 1: make no contribution. Plan 2: contribute $20,000 in cash to the partnership on December 28, 2026. Plan 3: contribute $20,000 in cash on January 10, 2027. Which plan, or plans, avoid gain recognition for 2026?","d":"","o":["Plans 2 and 3; a contribution before the return is filed restores basis, so there is no gain","Plan 2 only; Plans 1 and 3 each produce a $25,000 gain in 2026","Plan 2 only; Plans 1 and 3 each produce a $15,000 gain in 2026","None of the plans; the gain arises from the liability decrease and is $15,000 under any of them"],"a":3,"cite":"IRC §731(a)(1); IRC §752(b); IRC §705(a)","rat":"Basis is increased by income and contributions before it is reduced by distributions and the deemed distribution from the liability decrease.","ex":["Basis is measured at the end of the partnership's tax year. A contribution made after year-end cannot prevent gain for that year.","Forgets to increase basis by her $10,000 share of income. Distributions of $75,000 exceed basis of $60,000 by $15,000, not $25,000.","Basis is increased by income and contributions before it is reduced by distributions and the deemed distribution from the liability decrease. Before the contribution: $50,000 + $10,000 - $75,000 = -$15,000, so gain is $15,000. A December contribution of $20,000 brings basis to $5,000 and avoids gain. A January contribution comes too late for 2026.","Contributions increase basis, so a contribution before year-end can absorb the deemed distribution. The $20,000 more than covers the $15,000 shortfall."],"wt":["concept","calc","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"C","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A partner's basis is increased by income and contributions and then decreased by distributions, including deemed cash distributions from liability decreases; distributions above basis cause gain, and only contributions made by year-end count.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Basis before distributions = 50,000 + 10,000 = 60,000; distributions and deemed distribution = 40,000 + 35,000 = 75,000","Plan 1: gain = 75,000 - 60,000 = 15,000","Plan 2: basis = 60,000 + 20,000 = 80,000 >= 75,000, no gain; basis after = 5,000","Plan 3: contribution is in 2027, so the 2026 gain is 15,000"]}}],"26":[{"i":"TCP-II-C-0004","t":"A partnership borrows $300,000 to buy a building. Partners A, B and C share profits 50%, 30% and 20%. A is a limited partner. Consider two loan structures. In the first, the loan is nonrecourse with no partner personally liable and there is no minimum gain and no built-in gain property. In the second, the loan is recourse, and B alone guarantees it with no right of reimbursement. By how much is A's basis increase under the first structure greater than under the second?","mc":{"s":"A partnership borrows $300,000 to buy a building. Partners A, B and C share profits 50%, 30% and 20%. A is a limited partner. Consider two loan structures. In the first, the loan is nonrecourse with no partner personally liable and there is no minimum gain and no built-in gain property. In the second, the loan is recourse, and B alone guarantees it with no right of reimbursement. By how much is A's basis increase under the first structure greater than under the second?","d":"","o":["$60,000","$90,000","$100,000","$150,000"],"a":4,"cite":"IRC §752(a); Reg. §1.752-2; Reg. §1.752-3","rat":"For the nonrecourse loan the liability is shared by profit percentages, giving A 50% x $300,000 = $150,000.","ex":["Uses C's profit percentage, which is not A's share.","Uses B's profit percentage, which is not A's share.","Splits the loan equally among the three partners.","For the nonrecourse loan the liability is shared by profit percentages, giving A 50% x $300,000 = $150,000. For the recourse loan only B bears the economic risk of loss, so A's share is $0. The difference is $150,000."],"wt":["concept","concept","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"C","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Nonrecourse debt is shared by profit percentages; recourse debt is allocated to the partner who bears the economic risk of loss.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Nonrecourse: A's share = 50% x 300,000 = $150,000","Recourse guaranteed by B alone: A's share = $0","Difference = $150,000"]}},{"i":"TCP-II-C-0005","t":"Ola Park has a basis of $10,000 in her general partnership interest. In 2026 the partnership repays $60,000 of a recourse loan, and her share of that reduction is 30%. She has no other basis adjustments and receives no other distribution. What gain does she recognize?","mc":{"s":"Ola Park has a basis of $10,000 in her general partnership interest. In 2026 the partnership repays $60,000 of a recourse loan, and her share of that reduction is 30%. She has no other basis adjustments and receives no other distribution. What gain does she recognize?","d":"","o":["$8,000","$10,000","$18,000","$50,000"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §752(b); IRC §731(a)(1); Reg. §1.752-1(c)","rat":"Her share of the debt decrease is 30% x $60,000 = $18,000, a deemed cash distribution.","ex":["Her share of the debt decrease is 30% x $60,000 = $18,000, a deemed cash distribution. It exceeds her basis of $10,000 by $8,000, which is gain.","Reports her basis as the gain.","Treats the whole deemed distribution as gain. Basis is recovered first.","Uses the whole loan repayment instead of her 30% share."],"wt":["","calc","concept","calc"],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"C","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A partner's share of a liability decrease is a deemed distribution of cash that triggers gain only in excess of basis.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Deemed distribution = 30% x 60,000 = $18,000","Gain = $18,000 - 10,000 = $8,000"]}}],"27":[{"i":"TCP-II-C-0006","t":"Ari Volk owns 30% of the profits of a partnership. He also owns a building that he rents to the partnership for $24,000 a year at a fair rent. The partnership's 2026 ordinary income before deducting the rent is $200,000. How much total income does Ari report from the partnership and the rent?","mc":{"s":"Ari Volk owns 30% of the profits of a partnership. He also owns a building that he rents to the partnership for $24,000 a year at a fair rent. The partnership's 2026 ordinary income before deducting the rent is $200,000. How much total income does Ari report from the partnership and the rent?","d":"","o":["$52,800","$60,000","$76,800","$84,000"],"a":3,"cite":"IRC §707(a)(1); Reg. §1.707-1(a); IRC §702","rat":"The rent is a payment to Ari acting other than as a partner, so it is deductible by the partnership and taxed to him as rent.","ex":["Omits the rent Ari receives.","Ignores both the rent deduction and the rent income.","The rent is a payment to Ari acting other than as a partner, so it is deductible by the partnership and taxed to him as rent. His share of ordinary income is 30% x ($200,000 - $24,000) = $52,800. Total $76,800.","Adds the rent but fails to reduce the partnership's income for the rent it deducts."],"wt":["concept","concept","","calc"],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"C","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Payments to a partner in a non-partner capacity are treated as between the partnership and an outsider: deductible by the partnership and income to the partner.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Partnership income after rent = 200,000 - 24,000 = $176,000","Ari's share = 30% x $176,000 = $52,800","Total = 24,000 + $52,800 = $76,800"]}},{"i":"TCP-II-C-0013","t":"Vera Muñoz owns 60% of the capital and profits of Marlow Partners. She sells land that she held as an investment (basis $40,000) to the partnership for $100,000, its fair market value. The partnership will hold the land as inventory for resale. What is the amount and character of Vera's gain?","mc":{"s":"Vera Muñoz owns 60% of the capital and profits of Marlow Partners. She sells land that she held as an investment (basis $40,000) to the partnership for $100,000, its fair market value. The partnership will hold the land as inventory for resale. What is the amount and character of Vera's gain?","d":"","o":["$60,000 of ordinary income","$60,000 of long-term capital gain","$60,000 of section 1231 gain","$100,000 of ordinary income"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §707(b)(2); IRC §707(b)(1); IRC §1221","rat":"A gain on a sale between a partnership and a partner owning more than 50% is ordinary if the property is not a capital asset in the partnership's hands.","ex":["A gain on a sale between a partnership and a partner owning more than 50% is ordinary if the property is not a capital asset in the partnership's hands. The gain is $100,000 - $40,000 = $60,000, ordinary.","Would apply if the partnership held the land as a capital asset. Here it holds it as inventory.","Section 1231 does not apply because the property is inventory to the buyer, so the ordinary character rule controls.","Treats the whole price as ordinary income. Only the gain is income."],"wt":["","concept","concept","calc"],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"C","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Gain on a sale of property between a partnership and a more-than-50% partner is ordinary if the property is not a capital asset to the transferee.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Gain = 100,000 - 40,000 = $60,000","Character: ordinary because the partnership holds the land as inventory"]}}],"28":[{"i":"TCP-II-C-0007","t":"Ben Colby contributes land with an adjusted basis of $30,000 and a fair market value of $90,000 to a partnership in which he has a 40% share of profits and Dee has a 60% share. The partnership later sells the land for $150,000. How much of the gain on the sale is allocated to Ben?","mc":{"s":"Ben Colby contributes land with an adjusted basis of $30,000 and a fair market value of $90,000 to a partnership in which he has a 40% share of profits and Dee has a 60% share. The partnership later sells the land for $150,000. How much of the gain on the sale is allocated to Ben?","d":"","o":["$48,000","$60,000","$84,000","$120,000"],"a":3,"cite":"IRC §704(c)(1)(A); Reg. §1.704-3(a); IRC §721","rat":"The built-in gain at contribution, $90,000 - $30,000 = $60,000, is allocated to Ben.","ex":["Shares the whole gain by profit percentage and ignores that the built-in gain belongs to the contributor.","Allocates only the built-in gain and omits his share of the later appreciation.","The built-in gain at contribution, $90,000 - $30,000 = $60,000, is allocated to Ben. The remaining gain of $150,000 - $90,000 = $60,000 is shared 40% to Ben, giving him $24,000. Total $84,000.","Allocates the entire gain to Ben."],"wt":["concept","calc","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"C","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Built-in gain on contributed property is allocated to the contributing partner; post-contribution gain is shared according to the partnership agreement.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Built-in gain = 90,000 - 30,000 = $60,000","Remaining gain = 150,000 - 90,000 = $60,000; Ben's 40% = $24,000","Total = $84,000"]}},{"i":"TCP-II-C-0014","t":"Alpha contributes equipment with a tax basis of $40,000 and a value of $100,000 to a new partnership, and Beta contributes $100,000 of cash. The partners share all items equally. The equipment has a 10-year remaining life and is depreciated straight-line for both book and tax, so book depreciation is $10,000 a year and tax depreciation is $4,000 a year. Compare the traditional method with the remedial method for allocating the equipment's tax depreciation in the first year. Which statement is correct?","mc":{"s":"Alpha contributes equipment with a tax basis of $40,000 and a value of $100,000 to a new partnership, and Beta contributes $100,000 of cash. The partners share all items equally. The equipment has a 10-year remaining life and is depreciated straight-line for both book and tax, so book depreciation is $10,000 a year and tax depreciation is $4,000 a year. Compare the traditional method with the remedial method for allocating the equipment's tax depreciation in the first year. Which statement is correct?","d":"","o":["Under the remedial method Beta's taxable income is $1,000 lower and Alpha's is unchanged from the traditional method","Under the remedial method Beta's taxable income is unchanged and Alpha's is $1,000 higher than under the traditional method","Under the remedial method Beta's taxable income is $1,000 higher and Alpha's is $1,000 lower than under the traditional method","Under the remedial method Beta's taxable income is $1,000 lower and Alpha's is $1,000 higher than under the traditional method"],"a":4,"cite":"IRC §704(c)(1)(A); Reg. §1.704-3(b); Reg. §1.704-3(d)","rat":"Beta's book share of depreciation is $5,000, but the ceiling rule limits tax depreciation to the $4,000 available, all of which goes to Beta under the traditional method.","ex":["The remedial method creates an offsetting item for the contributing partner. Alpha reports $1,000 of remedial income, so Alpha's income is not unchanged.","Beta is the partner who benefits from the remedial deduction, so her income falls by $1,000. Both partners are affected.","Reverses the effect. The remedial method corrects Beta's shortfall of depreciation, so Beta gets the deduction and Alpha gets the income.","Beta's book share of depreciation is $5,000, but the ceiling rule limits tax depreciation to the $4,000 available, all of which goes to Beta under the traditional method. The remedial method gives Beta a $1,000 additional deduction and Alpha $1,000 of offsetting income."],"wt":["concept","concept","trap",""],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"C","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Under the traditional method the ceiling rule can leave the noncontributing partner with less tax depreciation than book depreciation, while the remedial method fixes the shortfall with an offsetting deduction to that partner and income to the contributing partner.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Book depreciation = 100,000 / 10 = 10,000; each partner's book share = 5,000; tax depreciation = 40,000 / 10 = 4,000","Traditional: Beta gets all 4,000 of tax depreciation (ceiling rule), a shortfall of 5,000 - 4,000 = 1,000; Alpha gets 0","Remedial: Beta gets an additional 1,000 deduction and Alpha reports 1,000 of remedial income","Beta's income is 1,000 lower and Alpha's is 1,000 higher under the remedial method"]}}],"29":[{"i":"TCP-II-C-0008","t":"Hal Kirk has an outside basis of $40,000 in his partnership interest. In a current (nonliquidating) distribution the partnership distributes to him marketable securities with a fair market value of $60,000 and a partnership basis of $35,000, and no other property. The partnership is not an investment partnership, Hal did not contribute the securities, and his share of net appreciation in the partnership's marketable securities immediately before the distribution is $0. What gain does Hal recognize?","mc":{"s":"Hal Kirk has an outside basis of $40,000 in his partnership interest. In a current (nonliquidating) distribution the partnership distributes to him marketable securities with a fair market value of $60,000 and a partnership basis of $35,000, and no other property. The partnership is not an investment partnership, Hal did not contribute the securities, and his share of net appreciation in the partnership's marketable securities immediately before the distribution is $0. What gain does Hal recognize?","d":"","o":["$0","$20,000","$25,000","$60,000"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §731(c)(1); IRC §731(a)(1); IRC §731(c)(3)","rat":"Marketable securities distributed are treated as money to the extent of their fair market value.","ex":["Assumes a property distribution is tax-free within basis. Marketable securities are treated as cash for this purpose.","Marketable securities distributed are treated as money to the extent of their fair market value. The $60,000 deemed cash exceeds his outside basis of $40,000, so he recognizes gain of $20,000.","Measures gain against the partnership's basis in the securities instead of his outside basis.","Treats the whole value of the securities as gain and ignores his outside basis."],"wt":["concept","","concept","calc"],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"C","skill":"Application","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Marketable securities distributed by a partnership are treated as money, so gain arises to the extent their value exceeds the partner's outside basis.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Deemed money = 60,000","Gain = 60,000 - 40,000 = $20,000"]}},{"i":"TCP-II-C-0016","t":"In 2023 Dev contributed land with a basis of $50,000 and a value of $200,000 to the DevCo partnership for an interest. The partnership still holds the land. In 2026 the partnership makes a nonliquidating distribution to Dev of other property worth $180,000, in which the partnership's basis is $90,000. Dev's basis in his partnership interest immediately before the distribution is $100,000, and no cash is distributed. The partnership could instead make the same distribution in 2031, when the land is still held. By how much does waiting until 2031 reduce the gain Dev recognizes?","mc":{"s":"In 2023 Dev contributed land with a basis of $50,000 and a value of $200,000 to the DevCo partnership for an interest. The partnership still holds the land. In 2026 the partnership makes a nonliquidating distribution to Dev of other property worth $180,000, in which the partnership's basis is $90,000. Dev's basis in his partnership interest immediately before the distribution is $100,000, and no cash is distributed. The partnership could instead make the same distribution in 2031, when the land is still held. By how much does waiting until 2031 reduce the gain Dev recognizes?","d":"","o":["$30,000","$50,000","$80,000","$150,000"],"a":3,"cite":"IRC §737(a); IRC §737(b); IRC §731(a)","rat":"A distribution to a contributing partner of other property within seven years of the contribution triggers gain equal to the lesser of the excess of the value received over his basis ($180,000 - $100,000 = $80,000) or his net precontribution gain ($150,000).","ex":["Subtracts the built-in gain from the value received. The gain is measured by comparing the value received to his basis, not to the land's built-in gain.","This is the basis of the contributed land. The gain is measured by comparing the value received to his outside basis, and it is capped by the built-in gain.","A distribution to a contributing partner of other property within seven years of the contribution triggers gain equal to the lesser of the excess of the value received over his basis ($180,000 - $100,000 = $80,000) or his net precontribution gain ($150,000). Gain is $80,000 in 2026. In 2031, more than seven years have passed and a property distribution within his basis causes no gain.","This is the net precontribution gain, which is only a cap on the gain. The lesser amount, $80,000, is recognized."],"wt":["calc","concept","","trap"],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"C","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A partner who contributed appreciated property recognizes gain if the partnership distributes other property to him within seven years, equal to the lesser of the excess of the value received over his outside basis or his net precontribution gain.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Excess of value received over outside basis = 180,000 - 100,000 = 80,000","Net precontribution gain = 200,000 - 50,000 = 150,000","2026 gain = min(80,000, 150,000) = 80,000; a 2031 distribution is outside the seven-year period and within his basis, so gain = 0","Reduction = 80,000"]}}],"30":[{"i":"TCP-II-C-0009","t":"Sue Mott liquidates her entire partnership interest, in which her basis is $60,000. She receives $20,000 in cash, inventory with a partnership basis of $15,000 (fair market value $30,000), and land with a partnership basis of $10,000 (fair market value $50,000). There is no change in liabilities. What is her basis in the land?","mc":{"s":"Sue Mott liquidates her entire partnership interest, in which her basis is $60,000. She receives $20,000 in cash, inventory with a partnership basis of $15,000 (fair market value $30,000), and land with a partnership basis of $10,000 (fair market value $50,000). There is no change in liabilities. What is her basis in the land?","d":"","o":["$10,000","$15,000","$20,000","$25,000"],"a":4,"cite":"IRC §732(b); IRC §732(c); IRC §731(a)","rat":"In a liquidation the partner's outside basis is first reduced by cash ($20,000), then assigned to inventory at the partnership's basis ($15,000), and the remainder goes to the other property: $60,000 - $20,000 - $15,000 = $25,000.","ex":["Uses the partnership's basis in the land. In a liquidation, any remaining outside basis is assigned to the remaining property.","Also subtracts the land's own basis.","Splits the remaining basis equally between inventory and land.","In a liquidation the partner's outside basis is first reduced by cash ($20,000), then assigned to inventory at the partnership's basis ($15,000), and the remainder goes to the other property: $60,000 - $20,000 - $15,000 = $25,000."],"wt":["concept","calc","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"C","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"In a liquidating distribution outside basis is reduced by cash, allocated first to inventory and receivables at carryover basis, then to other property.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["After cash = 60,000 - 20,000 = $40,000","Inventory basis = 15,000","Land basis = $40,000 - 15,000 = $25,000"]}},{"i":"TCP-II-C-0017","t":"Dina Voss, a general partner, retires from Voss & Klein, an accounting partnership in which capital is not a material income-producing factor. Her basis in her partnership interest is $100,000. In liquidation of her entire interest the partnership will pay her $300,000: $100,000 for her share of the partnership's cash and other property and $200,000 that reflects her share of unrecorded goodwill. Plan 1: the partnership agreement, which does not mention goodwill, stays as it is. Plan 2: the partners amend the agreement before Dina retires to provide for payment for her share of goodwill. Dina's rates are 37% on ordinary income and 20% on capital gain. The remaining partners are in the 37% bracket and receive any deduction the partnership gets. Ignore other taxes. By how much does Plan 2 increase the combined federal income tax of Dina and the remaining partners compared with Plan 1?","mc":{"s":"Dina Voss, a general partner, retires from Voss & Klein, an accounting partnership in which capital is not a material income-producing factor. Her basis in her partnership interest is $100,000. In liquidation of her entire interest the partnership will pay her $300,000: $100,000 for her share of the partnership's cash and other property and $200,000 that reflects her share of unrecorded goodwill. Plan 1: the partnership agreement, which does not mention goodwill, stays as it is. Plan 2: the partners amend the agreement before Dina retires to provide for payment for her share of goodwill. Dina's rates are 37% on ordinary income and 20% on capital gain. The remaining partners are in the 37% bracket and receive any deduction the partnership gets. Ignore other taxes. By how much does Plan 2 increase the combined federal income tax of Dina and the remaining partners compared with Plan 1?","d":"","o":["$0","$34,000","$40,000","$74,000"],"a":3,"cite":"IRC §736(a)(2); IRC §736(b)(3); IRC §731(a); IRC §707(c)","rat":"Plan 1: the $200,000 for goodwill is a payment under the ordinary income rules, taxed to Dina at 37% ($74,000) and deducted by the partnership, saving the remaining partners $74,000, for a net of $0.","ex":["This is the combined tax difference in Plan 1, where Dina's ordinary income is offset by the partners' deduction. Plan 2 adds capital gain tax with no offsetting deduction.","This is only Dina's tax saving from capital gain treatment ($34,000). It ignores that the partnership loses its $200,000 deduction.","Plan 1: the $200,000 for goodwill is a payment under the ordinary income rules, taxed to Dina at 37% ($74,000) and deducted by the partnership, saving the remaining partners $74,000, for a net of $0. Plan 2: the payment is a distribution for her interest, so she has $200,000 of capital gain ($40,000) and no deduction is available. Plan 2 increases combined tax by $40,000.","This is the value of the deduction lost by the remaining partners, without the tax Dina saves. Combined, Dina's tax falls by $34,000."],"wt":["concept","trap","","trap"],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"C","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"In a service partnership, a retiring partner's payment for goodwill that the agreement does not mention is an ordinary income payment that the partnership deducts; if the agreement provides for goodwill it is a property payment taxed as capital gain, with no deduction.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Plan 1: 200,000 is ordinary to Dina (74,000) and deductible by the partnership (saves 74,000); basis 100,000 is recovered by the 100,000 property payment","Plan 2: total payment 300,000 - basis 100,000 = 200,000 capital gain; tax = 200,000 x 20% = 40,000; no partnership deduction","Plan 1 combined = 74,000 - 74,000 = 0; Plan 2 combined = 40,000; increase = 40,000"]}}],"31":[{"i":"TCP-II-C-0010","t":"Eva Lin owned 40% of a calendar-year partnership until September 30, 2026, when she sold half of her interest, leaving her with a 20% interest. The partnership earned $300,000 of ordinary income ratably during 2026, and the partners agreed to allocate by proration. What is Eva's distributive share of the 2026 income?","mc":{"s":"Eva Lin owned 40% of a calendar-year partnership until September 30, 2026, when she sold half of her interest, leaving her with a 20% interest. The partnership earned $300,000 of ordinary income ratably during 2026, and the partners agreed to allocate by proration. What is Eva's distributive share of the 2026 income?","d":"","o":["$60,000","$75,000","$90,000","$105,000"],"a":4,"cite":"IRC §706(d)(1); Reg. §1.706-4; IRC §704(b)","rat":"For the first nine months she held 40%: $300,000 x 9/12 x 40% = $90,000.","ex":["Uses 20% for the whole year. She held 40% for most of it.","Reverses the two ownership percentages.","Counts only the first nine months at 40%.","For the first nine months she held 40%: $300,000 x 9/12 x 40% = $90,000. For the last three months she held 20%: $300,000 x 3/12 x 20% = $15,000. Total $105,000."],"wt":["concept","calc","calc",""],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"C","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"When a partner's interest varies during the year, distributive share is determined by the portion of the year each interest was held.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["First nine months = 300,000 x 0.75 x 40% = $90,000","Last three months = 300,000 x 0.25 x 20% = $15,000","Total = $105,000"]}}],"32":[{"i":"TCP-II-C-0011","t":"Gus Amato buys a 25% partnership interest from another partner for $150,000. The partnership's assets have a total inside basis of $400,000 and include land with a basis of $100,000 and a fair market value of $300,000. The other assets have a fair market value equal to their basis. Immediately after Gus's purchase the partnership sells the land for $300,000. By how much does an election to adjust the basis of partnership assets on transfers, in effect at the time of the purchase, reduce the gain Gus reports from the sale?","mc":{"s":"Gus Amato buys a 25% partnership interest from another partner for $150,000. The partnership's assets have a total inside basis of $400,000 and include land with a basis of $100,000 and a fair market value of $300,000. The other assets have a fair market value equal to their basis. Immediately after Gus's purchase the partnership sells the land for $300,000. By how much does an election to adjust the basis of partnership assets on transfers, in effect at the time of the purchase, reduce the gain Gus reports from the sale?","d":"","o":["$25,000","$50,000","$100,000","$150,000"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §743(b); IRC §754; IRC §755","rat":"Without the election, Gus reports his 25% share of the partnership's $200,000 gain, or $50,000.","ex":["Uses half of his share of the gain. The adjustment fully offsets his share here.","Without the election, Gus reports his 25% share of the partnership's $200,000 gain, or $50,000. With the election, the section 743(b) adjustment of $150,000 - (25% x $400,000) = $50,000 is allocated to the land, which eliminates that gain. The reduction is $50,000.","This is his share of inside basis, not the adjustment.","Uses the purchase price, which is not the basis adjustment."],"wt":["calc","","trap","trap"],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"C","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A section 754 election gives a purchasing partner an inside basis adjustment equal to outside basis minus share of inside basis, allocated to appreciated assets.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Share of inside basis = 25% x 400,000 = $100,000","743(b) adjustment = 150,000 - $100,000 = $50,000","Gain without election = 25% x $200,000 = $50,000; with election = $0"]}}],"33":[{"i":"TCP-II-C-0012","t":"Which statement about the partnership election to adjust the basis of partnership property on transfers of interests is correct?","mc":{"s":"Which statement about the partnership election to adjust the basis of partnership property on transfers of interests is correct?","d":"","o":["It is made separately by each transferee partner on a Form 8832 filed with the partnership's return each year","It applies only to the year in which it is made and must be made again with the return each year to continue","It applies only to transfers of interests at death and not to sales of interests or to partnership distributions","Once made, it applies to later transfers and to distributions until the IRS consents to revoke it"],"a":4,"cite":"IRC §754; Reg. §1.754-1(b); IRC §743(a)","rat":"The election is made by the partnership with a timely filed return and stays in effect for all later transfers and distributions.","ex":["The election is made by the partnership, not by an individual partner, and Form 8832 is an entity classification election.","The election continues to apply to later years until revoked with IRS consent.","The election also applies to sales and to certain distributions.","The election is made by the partnership with a timely filed return and stays in effect for all later transfers and distributions."],"wt":["concept","concept","concept",""],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"C","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A section 754 election is made by the partnership on a timely return and applies to transfers of interests and distributions in that and all later years.","from":"2026-01-01"}}],"34":[{"i":"TCP-II-D-0001","t":"The Wren Family Trust files a 2026 Form 1041. Which set of facts makes it a simple trust for 2026? The trust makes no charitable payments in any of the cases.","mc":{"s":"The Wren Family Trust files a 2026 Form 1041. Which set of facts makes it a simple trust for 2026? The trust makes no charitable payments in any of the cases.","d":"","o":["The trustee may accumulate income or distribute it to beneficiaries at the trustee's discretion","The trust instrument requires all income to be distributed currently, and no principal is distributed during the year","The instrument requires all income to be distributed currently, and the trustee also distributes $10,000 of principal to a beneficiary during the year","The grantor retains the power to revoke the trust, and all income is distributed currently to the beneficiary"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §651(a); IRC §661; IRC §671","rat":"A simple trust must distribute all income currently, may not make principal distributions or charitable payments, and is not a grantor trust.","ex":["A trust that may accumulate income is a complex trust.","A simple trust must distribute all income currently, may not make principal distributions or charitable payments, and is not a grantor trust.","A trust that distributes principal in the year is a complex trust for that year.","A trust the grantor can revoke is a grantor trust, whose income is reported by the grantor rather than as a simple trust."],"wt":["concept","","trap","concept"],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"D","skill":"Application","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A simple trust must distribute all income currently, make no principal or charitable distributions, and not be a grantor trust; otherwise it is complex or grantor.","from":"2026-01-01"}}],"35":[{"i":"TCP-II-D-0002","t":"Which statement about the taxation of a trust's income that is distributed to beneficiaries is correct?","mc":{"s":"Which statement about the taxation of a trust's income that is distributed to beneficiaries is correct?","d":"","o":["The trust deducts every distribution it makes, including distributions of principal beyond distributable net income","The trust pays tax on all of its income, and beneficiaries exclude distributions from income as gifts","The trust deducts distributions up to distributable net income, and beneficiaries include them in income","The beneficiaries are taxed only on distributions in excess of distributable net income"],"a":3,"cite":"IRC §661(a); IRC §662(a); IRC §643(a)","rat":"The distribution deduction is limited to DNI, which also limits what beneficiaries include in income.","ex":["The distribution deduction cannot exceed distributable net income.","Distributions of income are taxed to the beneficiaries to the extent of distributable net income and are not gifts.","The distribution deduction is limited to DNI, which also limits what beneficiaries include in income.","The opposite is true: distributions up to DNI are taxed to the beneficiaries; the excess is not."],"wt":["limit","concept","","trap"],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"D","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A trust is a conduit to the extent of DNI: it deducts distributions up to DNI and beneficiaries include them, retaining the character of the income.","from":"2026-01-01"}}],"36":[{"i":"TCP-II-D-0003","t":"Carol Reed created a trust in 2020 and transferred property to it. She kept the power to revoke the trust and to withdraw the assets at any time. Which statement about the trust in 2026 is correct?","mc":{"s":"Carol Reed created a trust in 2020 and transferred property to it. She kept the power to revoke the trust and to withdraw the assets at any time. Which statement about the trust in 2026 is correct?","d":"","o":["The trust is a separate taxpayer that files its own return and takes a personal exemption of $300 a year","The transfer to the trust was a completed gift when made and is subject to federal gift tax at that time","The trust assets are excluded from Carol's gross estate because legal title is held by an independent trustee","Its income is taxed to Carol, and its assets are in her gross estate if she dies while it is revocable"],"a":4,"cite":"IRC §676(a); IRC §671; IRC §2038(a)(1)","rat":"A revocable trust is a grantor trust, so its income is taxed to the grantor, and the retained power to revoke brings the assets into the estate.","ex":["A revocable trust is disregarded as a separate taxpayer; the grantor reports the income.","Because Carol kept the power to revoke, the transfer was not a completed gift.","Legal title does not matter. The retained power to revoke causes estate inclusion.","A revocable trust is a grantor trust, so its income is taxed to the grantor, and the retained power to revoke brings the assets into the estate."],"wt":["concept","concept","trap",""],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"D","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A revocable trust is a grantor trust: the grantor reports its income, the transfer is an incomplete gift, and the assets are in the gross estate.","from":"2026-01-01"}}],"37":[{"i":"TCP-II-D-0004","t":"The Hollis Trust received $12,000 of cash dividends and $5,000 of interest in 2026 and sold stock at a $30,000 gain. It paid $3,000 of regular trustee compensation. Assume the state has adopted the Uniform Principal and Income Act: cash dividends and interest are income, capital gains are principal, and regular trustee compensation is charged half to income and half to principal. What is the trust's fiduciary accounting income for 2026?","mc":{"s":"The Hollis Trust received $12,000 of cash dividends and $5,000 of interest in 2026 and sold stock at a $30,000 gain. It paid $3,000 of regular trustee compensation. Assume the state has adopted the Uniform Principal and Income Act: cash dividends and interest are income, capital gains are principal, and regular trustee compensation is charged half to income and half to principal. What is the trust's fiduciary accounting income for 2026?","d":"","o":["$5,000","$12,000","$14,000","$15,500"],"a":4,"cite":"IRC §643(b); Reg. §1.643(b)-1; UPIA §401, §501","rat":"Income consists of dividends and interest ($17,000) less half of the trustee compensation charged to income ($1,500): $15,500.","ex":["Counts only the interest and leaves out the dividends.","Counts only the dividends and leaves out the interest.","Charges all of the trustee compensation to income, but only half is charged to income.","Income consists of dividends and interest ($17,000) less half of the trustee compensation charged to income ($1,500): $15,500. The capital gain is principal."],"wt":["concept","concept","calc",""],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"D","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Fiduciary accounting income includes dividends and interest, excludes capital gains, and is reduced by expenses charged to income under state law.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Receipts = 12,000 + 5,000 = $17,000","Expenses charged to income = 50% x 3,000 = $1,500","Accounting income = $15,500"]}}],"38":[{"i":"TCP-II-D-0005","t":"The Marsh Trust is a complex trust. In 2026 it had $20,000 of dividends and $10,000 of interest, and a $15,000 capital gain that the trustee allocated to corpus and retained. It paid $4,000 of trustee fees, all charged to income and deductible. The trustee distributed $20,000 that the instrument required to Ann and $10,000 in a discretionary distribution to Bill. The trust's exemption is $100. What is the trust's 2026 taxable income?","mc":{"s":"The Marsh Trust is a complex trust. In 2026 it had $20,000 of dividends and $10,000 of interest, and a $15,000 capital gain that the trustee allocated to corpus and retained. It paid $4,000 of trustee fees, all charged to income and deductible. The trustee distributed $20,000 that the instrument required to Ann and $10,000 in a discretionary distribution to Bill. The trust's exemption is $100. What is the trust's 2026 taxable income?","d":"","o":["$10,900","$14,900","$15,000","$40,900"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §661(a); IRC §643(a); IRC §642(b)(2)(A)","rat":"Distributable net income is $20,000 + $10,000 - $4,000 = $26,000; the corpus capital gain is excluded.","ex":["Deducts the full distributions. The deduction is limited to distributable net income.","Distributable net income is $20,000 + $10,000 - $4,000 = $26,000; the corpus capital gain is excluded. The distributions ($30,000) exceed DNI, so the deduction is $26,000. Taxable income = $41,000 - $26,000 - $100 = $14,900.","Leaves out the trust's $100 exemption.","Takes no distribution deduction at all."],"wt":["limit","","calc","concept"],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"D","skill":"Application","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A trust's taxable income equals its income less deductions, the distribution deduction (limited to DNI), and its exemption.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Total income less fees = $41,000","DNI = $26,000 (capital gain to corpus excluded)","Distribution deduction = lesser of $30,000 and $26,000 = $26,000","Taxable income = $41,000 - $26,000 - 100 = $14,900"]}},{"i":"TCP-II-D-0006","t":"The Vale Trust has $60,000 of taxable income before any distribution deduction and exemption. Assume the trust's tax is $3,000 on the first $16,000 of income and 37% on the rest if it keeps the income. The trust's sole beneficiary is in the 24% bracket. The trustee can either retain the income or distribute all of it currently; distributable net income equals $60,000. Ignoring the trust exemption, what federal income tax does the family save by distributing the income?","mc":{"s":"The Vale Trust has $60,000 of taxable income before any distribution deduction and exemption. Assume the trust's tax is $3,000 on the first $16,000 of income and 37% on the rest if it keeps the income. The trust's sole beneficiary is in the 24% bracket. The trustee can either retain the income or distribute all of it currently; distributable net income equals $60,000. Ignoring the trust exemption, what federal income tax does the family save by distributing the income?","d":"","o":["$4,880","$7,800","$14,400","$19,280"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §661(a); IRC §662(a); IRC §1(e)","rat":"If the trust retains the income it pays $3,000 + 37% x ($60,000 - $16,000) = $19,280.","ex":["If the trust retains the income it pays $3,000 + 37% x ($60,000 - $16,000) = $19,280. If it distributes all income, the beneficiary pays 24% x $60,000 = $14,400 and the trust owes nothing. The saving is $4,880.","Assumes all retained income is taxed at 37%. The first $16,000 is taxed at lower amounts.","This is the beneficiary's tax on the distribution, not the saving.","This is the trust's tax if it keeps the income, not the saving."],"wt":["","calc","trap","trap"],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"D","skill":"Analysis","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Distributing income carries it out to beneficiaries, often at lower rates than the compressed trust brackets.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Trust tax if retained = 3,000 + 0.37 x 44,000 = $19,280","Beneficiary tax if distributed = 24% x 60,000 = $14,400","Saving = $4,880"]}},{"i":"TCP-II-D-0007","t":"The Larch Trust is a complex trust with 2026 distributable net income of $50,000, all ordinary income. The trustee paid $10,000 to beneficiary Beth during 2026 and will pay her $30,000 more on February 20, 2027. The trust has no 2027 income. Assume the trust's taxable income is taxed at a flat 37%, Beth is taxed at a flat 22% in both years, and ignore the trust's exemption. The trustee is considering whether to elect to treat the February 2027 payment as made on the last day of 2026. How much combined federal income tax (trust and Beth) does the election save?","mc":{"s":"The Larch Trust is a complex trust with 2026 distributable net income of $50,000, all ordinary income. The trustee paid $10,000 to beneficiary Beth during 2026 and will pay her $30,000 more on February 20, 2027. The trust has no 2027 income. Assume the trust's taxable income is taxed at a flat 37%, Beth is taxed at a flat 22% in both years, and ignore the trust's exemption. The trustee is considering whether to elect to treat the February 2027 payment as made on the last day of 2026. How much combined federal income tax (trust and Beth) does the election save?","d":"","o":["$4,500","$6,600","$11,100","$17,000"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §663(b); Reg. §1.663(b)-1; IRC §661(a); IRC §662(a)","rat":"Without the election, the trust is taxed on $40,000 (income of $50,000 less the $10,000 paid), $14,800, and Beth on $10,000, $2,200: total $17,000.","ex":["Without the election, the trust is taxed on $40,000 (income of $50,000 less the $10,000 paid), $14,800, and Beth on $10,000, $2,200: total $17,000. With the election, distributions for 2026 are $40,000, so the trust is taxed on $10,000 ($3,700) and Beth on $40,000 ($8,800): total $12,500. The savings are $4,500.","This is only the tax Beth pays on the extra $30,000. The trust saves more tax at 37% than Beth costs at 22%, and the net saving is what matters.","This is only the trust's tax saving. The election makes Beth taxable on the $30,000, which costs her $6,600.","This is the combined tax without the election ($17,000), not the amount saved by the election."],"wt":["","trap","trap","calc"],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"D","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A trustee can elect to treat distributions made within 65 days after year-end as made on the last day of the year, so that the amount is carried out to the beneficiary and deducted by the trust, limited to distributable net income not already distributed.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["No election: trust taxable = 50,000 - 10,000 = 40,000, tax = 14,800; Beth tax on 10,000 = 2,200; total 17,000","Election: distribution deduction = 10,000 + 30,000 = 40,000 (not above DNI 50,000); trust tax = 10,000 x 37% = 3,700; Beth tax = 40,000 x 22% = 8,800; total 12,500","Savings = 17,000 - 12,500 = 4,500"]}}],"39":[{"i":"TCP-II-E-0001","t":"Which organization is most likely to qualify for federal income tax exemption as a charitable organization?","mc":{"s":"Which organization is most likely to qualify for federal income tax exemption as a charitable organization?","d":"","o":["A nonprofit organized and operated only to provide free legal services to low-income people","An association of local car dealers organized to promote the interests of the automobile industry and of its dealer members","A social club providing recreation to its members, funded by member dues and food and drink sales at the clubhouse","A corporation organized to support and endorse candidates for public office in local, state, and national elections"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §501(c)(3); Reg. §1.501(c)(3)-1(a); Reg. §1.501(c)(3)-1(c)","rat":"A charitable organization must be organized and operated exclusively for exempt purposes, and no net earnings may benefit private individuals.","ex":["A charitable organization must be organized and operated exclusively for exempt purposes, and no net earnings may benefit private individuals.","A business league promotes the common business interests of members and is exempt under a different provision.","A social club serves its members' personal enjoyment and is exempt under a different provision.","Participating in a political campaign is not permitted for a charitable organization."],"wt":["","concept","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"E","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A 501(c)(3) organization must be organized and operated exclusively for exempt purposes, with no private inurement and no political campaign activity.","from":"2026-01-01"}}],"40":[{"i":"TCP-II-E-0002","t":"A public charity has operated a food bank for many years. Which event would most likely cause it to lose its tax-exempt status?","mc":{"s":"A public charity has operated a food bank for many years. Which event would most likely cause it to lose its tax-exempt status?","d":"","o":["The charity pays its executive director a reasonable salary for her full-time services to the food bank each year","The charity's board pays itself a large share of the net income each year instead of using it for the food bank","The charity earns 15% of its gross income from an unrelated trade or business that is not substantial","The charity spends a small part of its activities on lobbying for legislation related to hunger"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §501(c)(3); IRC §501(h); Reg. §1.501(c)(3)-1(c)(2)","rat":"Private inurement, where net earnings benefit insiders, is prohibited and can result in revocation of exemption.","ex":["Reasonable compensation for services is not private inurement.","Private inurement, where net earnings benefit insiders, is prohibited and can result in revocation of exemption.","An insubstantial unrelated business does not cost exemption; the income is subject to unrelated business income tax.","An insubstantial amount of lobbying is permitted for a charity."],"wt":["concept","","limit","limit"],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"E","skill":"Application","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"A charity loses exemption if it engages in private inurement, substantial non-exempt activity, or political campaign activity.","from":"2026-01-01"}}],"41":[{"i":"TCP-II-E-0003","t":"Which activity of a tax-exempt organization is most likely to produce unrelated business income?","mc":{"s":"Which activity of a tax-exempt organization is most likely to produce unrelated business income?","d":"","o":["A charity selling donated clothing in a thrift shop that is staffed entirely by unpaid volunteers of the charity","A museum selling catalogs and reproductions of the works in its own permanent collection in its gift shop","A university regularly operating a commercial bakery that sells to the general public","An educational charity receiving dividends and interest from its investment portfolio of stocks and bonds"],"a":3,"cite":"IRC §512(a)(1); IRC §513(a); IRC §512(b)","rat":"A regularly carried on trade or business that is not substantially related to the exempt purpose produces unrelated business income.","ex":["Sales of donated merchandise by volunteers are excluded from unrelated business income.","Sales of items closely related to the museum's exempt purpose are related business income.","A regularly carried on trade or business that is not substantially related to the exempt purpose produces unrelated business income.","Dividends, interest, and similar passive income are excluded from unrelated business income."],"wt":["concept","concept","","concept"],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"E","skill":"Remembering & Understanding","diff":1},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Unrelated business income comes from a regularly carried on trade or business not substantially related to the exempt purpose, subject to exclusions.","from":"2026-01-01"}},{"i":"TCP-II-E-0004","t":"Riverstone Foundation, a tax-exempt organization, owns a building it leases to a for-profit tenant for purposes unrelated to its exempt activities. It bought the building for $1,000,000, financed in part with a loan. For 2026 the average acquisition indebtedness is $400,000 and the average adjusted basis is $1,000,000. Rent is $150,000 and directly related expenses, including depreciation, are $50,000. It has no other unrelated business activity and takes the $1,000 specific deduction. What is its unrelated business taxable income?","mc":{"s":"Riverstone Foundation, a tax-exempt organization, owns a building it leases to a for-profit tenant for purposes unrelated to its exempt activities. It bought the building for $1,000,000, financed in part with a loan. For 2026 the average acquisition indebtedness is $400,000 and the average adjusted basis is $1,000,000. Rent is $150,000 and directly related expenses, including depreciation, are $50,000. It has no other unrelated business activity and takes the $1,000 specific deduction. What is its unrelated business taxable income?","d":"","o":["$39,000","$40,000","$99,000","$100,000"],"a":1,"cite":"IRC §514(a); IRC §512(b)(4); IRC §512(b)(12)","rat":"The debt-financed percentage is $400,000/$1,000,000 = 40%.","ex":["The debt-financed percentage is $400,000/$1,000,000 = 40%. Net rent is $150,000 - $50,000 = $100,000, of which 40% ($40,000) is unrelated debt-financed income. Less the $1,000 specific deduction: $39,000.","Omits the $1,000 specific deduction.","Includes all of the net rent instead of the debt-financed percentage.","Includes all of the net rent and omits both the debt-financed percentage and the specific deduction."],"wt":["","concept","concept","concept"],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"E","skill":"Application","diff":2},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Income from debt-financed property is unrelated business income in proportion to average acquisition indebtedness over average adjusted basis.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Debt-financed percentage = 400,000 / 1,000,000 = 40%","Net rent = 150,000 - 50,000 = $100,000; 40% = $40,000","UBTI = $40,000 - 1,000 = $39,000"]}},{"i":"TCP-II-E-0005","t":"Cedar Hall, a tax-exempt museum organized as a corporation, buys an office building for $2,000,000 using $1,200,000 borrowed from a bank at 6% interest and its own reserves, and leases it to a for-profit tenant. The lease is not related to the museum's exempt purpose. The building's average adjusted basis for the year is $2,000,000. Annual rent is $220,000, and straight-line depreciation and other expenses directly connected with the building, not counting interest, total $60,000. Plan A: keep the loan outstanding all year. Plan B: on January 1, repay $600,000 of the loan using reserves that are not debt-financed, so average acquisition indebtedness for the year is $600,000 and interest for the year is $36,000. Cedar Hall is taxed at 21% on unrelated business taxable income and has the $1,000 specific deduction. How much less tax does Plan B produce than Plan A?","mc":{"s":"Cedar Hall, a tax-exempt museum organized as a corporation, buys an office building for $2,000,000 using $1,200,000 borrowed from a bank at 6% interest and its own reserves, and leases it to a for-profit tenant. The lease is not related to the museum's exempt purpose. The building's average adjusted basis for the year is $2,000,000. Annual rent is $220,000, and straight-line depreciation and other expenses directly connected with the building, not counting interest, total $60,000. Plan A: keep the loan outstanding all year. Plan B: on January 1, repay $600,000 of the loan using reserves that are not debt-financed, so average acquisition indebtedness for the year is $600,000 and interest for the year is $36,000. Cedar Hall is taxed at 21% on unrelated business taxable income and has the $1,000 specific deduction. How much less tax does Plan B produce than Plan A?","d":"","o":["$2,520","$3,276","$5,439","$10,080"],"a":2,"cite":"IRC §514(a); Reg. §1.514(a)-1(a); IRC §514(c)(1); IRC §11(b)","rat":"Unrelated debt-financed income is the net income from the property multiplied by average acquisition indebtedness over average adjusted basis, and the directly connected deductions, including interest on the acquisition debt, are allowed in the same proportion.","ex":["Deducts all of the interest instead of the debt/basis percentage of it. Interest is one of the deductions that is allowed only in the same percentage as the income.","Unrelated debt-financed income is the net income from the property multiplied by average acquisition indebtedness over average adjusted basis, and the directly connected deductions, including interest on the acquisition debt, are allowed in the same proportion. Plan A: 60% x ($220,000 - $60,000 - $72,000) = $52,800, less $1,000, taxed at 21% is $10,878. Plan B: 30% x ($220,000 - $60,000 - $36,000) = $37,200, less $1,000, taxed at 21% is $7,602. The saving is $3,276.","Assumes that repaying half the debt halves the tax. The interest deduction also falls, and the percentage applies to a different net income, so the saving is smaller than half of Plan A's tax.","Leaves out the interest deduction. Interest on the acquisition debt is a directly connected deduction, allowed in the debt/basis percentage, and it is lower in Plan B."],"wt":["concept","","calc","trap"],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"E","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"Income from debt-financed property is unrelated business income in the proportion that average acquisition indebtedness bears to average adjusted basis, and directly connected deductions, including interest on the debt, are allowed in the same proportion.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Plan A: net income = 220,000 - 60,000 - 72,000 = 88,000; 60% x 88,000 = 52,800; UBTI = 51,800; tax = 10,878","Plan B: net income = 220,000 - 60,000 - 36,000 = 124,000; 30% x 124,000 = 37,200; UBTI = 36,200; tax = 7,602","Saving = 10,878 - 7,602 = 3,276"]}},{"i":"TCP-II-E-0006","t":"Harbor Arts Society, a charitable organization exempt from federal income tax, publishes a members' magazine on subjects related to its exempt purpose. For the year it has $90,000 of gross advertising income, $30,000 of costs directly connected with selling the advertising, $40,000 of circulation income, and $100,000 of readership costs (editorial and circulation costs). A sales campaign could raise advertising income by another $50,000 with no added costs. By how much would the campaign raise the society's unrelated business taxable income before the specific deduction?","mc":{"s":"Harbor Arts Society, a charitable organization exempt from federal income tax, publishes a members' magazine on subjects related to its exempt purpose. For the year it has $90,000 of gross advertising income, $30,000 of costs directly connected with selling the advertising, $40,000 of circulation income, and $100,000 of readership costs (editorial and circulation costs). A sales campaign could raise advertising income by another $50,000 with no added costs. By how much would the campaign raise the society's unrelated business taxable income before the specific deduction?","d":"","o":["$0","$10,000","$50,000","$110,000"],"a":3,"cite":"IRC §512(a)(1); Reg. §1.512(a)-1(f)(2); Reg. §1.512(a)-1(f)(3)","rat":"Advertising is unrelated income.","ex":["Assumes the readership costs continue to absorb the extra income. The deduction for readership costs above circulation income is capped at $60,000.","Deducts the entire $100,000 of readership costs. Circulation income of $40,000 offsets part of those costs, so only $60,000 is deductible against advertising.","Advertising is unrelated income. Readership costs are deductible against it only to the extent they exceed circulation income ($100,000 - $40,000 = $60,000), and no more than the net advertising income. Now: net advertising $60,000, deduction $60,000, UBTI $0. After: net advertising $110,000, deduction still $60,000, UBTI $50,000. The increase is $50,000.","Ignores the readership cost deduction entirely. Readership costs above circulation income are deductible against advertising income."],"wt":["concept","calc","","trap"],"tags":{"area":"II","group":"E","skill":"Analysis","diff":3},"asof":"2026-09-29","rl":"For an exempt organization's periodical, net advertising income is unrelated business income, reduced by readership costs only to the extent they exceed circulation income and only up to the net advertising income.","from":"2026-01-01","calc":1,"steps":["Readership costs in excess of circulation income = 100,000 - 40,000 = 60,000","Now: net advertising = 90,000 - 30,000 = 60,000; deduction = min(60,000, 60,000) = 60,000; UBTI = 0","After: net advertising = 140,000 - 30,000 = 110,000; deduction = min(110,000, 60,000) = 60,000; UBTI = 50,000","Increase = 50,000"]}}]};
window.PAIRS=[];
